1
00:00:01,435 --> 00:00:04,538
<i>♪ In the not too distant future ♪</i>

2
00:00:04,605 --> 00:00:07,474
<i>♪ Way down in Deep 13 ♪</i>

3
00:00:07,541 --> 00:00:13,180
<i>♪ Dr. Forrester and TV's Frank</i>
<i>Were hatching an evil scheme ♪</i>

4
00:00:13,847 --> 00:00:16,850
<i>♪ They hired a temp by the name of Mike ♪</i>

5
00:00:16,917 --> 00:00:20,020
<i>♪ Just a regular Joe they didn't like ♪</i>

6
00:00:20,087 --> 00:00:23,056
<i>♪ Their experiment needed</i>
<i>A good test case ♪</i>

7
00:00:23,123 --> 00:00:26,860
<i>♪ So they conked him on the noggin</i>
<i>And they shot him into space ♪</i>

8
00:00:26,927 --> 00:00:29,329
[Mike] Get me down!

9
00:00:29,396 --> 00:00:34,568
<i>♪ We'll send him cheesy movies</i>
<i>The worst we can find ♪</i>

10
00:00:34,635 --> 00:00:35,636
<i>♪ La-la-la ♪</i>

11
00:00:35,702 --> 00:00:41,041
<i>♪ He'll have to sit and watch them all</i>
<i>And we'll monitor his mind ♪</i>

12
00:00:41,108 --> 00:00:42,109
<i>♪ La-la-la ♪</i>

13
00:00:42,175 --> 00:00:44,945
<i>♪ Now keep in mind</i>
<i>Mike can't control ♪</i>

14
00:00:45,012 --> 00:00:47,014
<i>♪ Where the movies begin or end ♪</i>

15
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:48,081
<i>♪ La-la-la ♪</i>

16
00:00:48,148 --> 00:00:54,021
<i>♪ He'll try to keep his sanity</i>
<i>With the help of his robot friends ♪</i>

17
00:00:56,323 --> 00:00:57,691
Robot roll call.

18
00:00:57,758 --> 00:00:59,192
{\an8}-Cambot!
-Show yourself!

19
00:00:59,259 --> 00:01:00,460
{\an8}-Gypsy!
-I'm not ready!

20
00:01:00,527 --> 00:01:02,296
{\an8}-Tom Servo!
-Hello there!

21
00:01:02,362 --> 00:01:03,564
{\an8}-Crow!
-That's one "O".

22
00:01:03,630 --> 00:01:06,867
<i>♪ If you're wondering how</i>
<i>He eats and breathes ♪</i>

23
00:01:06,934 --> 00:01:08,902
<i>♪ And other science facts </i>♪

24
00:01:08,969 --> 00:01:09,970
<i>♪ La-la-la ♪</i>

25
00:01:10,037 --> 00:01:13,006
<i>♪ Just repeat to yourself</i>
<i>It's just a show ♪</i>

26
00:01:13,073 --> 00:01:15,943
<i>♪ I should really just relax ♪</i>

27
00:01:16,009 --> 00:01:21,348
<i>♪ For Mystery Science Theater 3000 ♪</i>

28
00:01:34,561 --> 00:01:37,664
-No, no. The plumb. Mike, the plum.
Mike, watch your plumb. Mike.

29
00:01:37,731 --> 00:01:39,299
-Yes, Servo, I know.

30
00:01:39,366 --> 00:01:42,035
-Uh, the pattern's
not matched up, Mike.

31
00:01:42,102 --> 00:01:44,738
Hey, you know, the pattern's not
matched up over there, either.

32
00:01:44,805 --> 00:01:46,873
-Thank you, Crow.
Thank you very much.

33
00:01:46,940 --> 00:01:48,008
Hi, everyone, I'm Mike.

34
00:01:48,075 --> 00:01:50,844
And, uh, Crow and Servo
here are helping me lots

35
00:01:50,911 --> 00:01:52,746
with my little
wallpapering project.

36
00:01:52,813 --> 00:01:54,815
-That's right. I thought this
would be a good way

37
00:01:54,881 --> 00:01:57,317
to give the place a
little perk me up. [laughs]

38
00:01:57,384 --> 00:01:59,386
We were gonna go with
the Christmas wallpaper.

39
00:01:59,453 --> 00:02:01,054
Then I put the kibosh on that.

40
00:02:01,121 --> 00:02:04,358
And it was very difficult to say
no to the birthday wallpaper,

41
00:02:04,424 --> 00:02:07,260
but I absolutely fell
in love with this.

42
00:02:07,327 --> 00:02:09,196
-Yes siree Bob,
we made the right choice.

43
00:02:09,262 --> 00:02:10,330
We gotta show Gypsy.

44
00:02:10,397 --> 00:02:12,666
Should we show Gypsy?
Hey, Gypsy!

45
00:02:12,733 --> 00:02:14,568
-Huh? Oh.

46
00:02:14,635 --> 00:02:15,636
Ooh.

47
00:02:15,702 --> 00:02:17,371
Oh, my God!

48
00:02:17,437 --> 00:02:20,641
Oh my, oh my, oh,
wrong, wrong, wrong!

49
00:02:20,707 --> 00:02:22,809
Oh, no! Oh, no! Oh, no!

50
00:02:22,876 --> 00:02:24,378
-[laughing] Oh, well.

51
00:02:24,444 --> 00:02:27,314
Sometimes, a new look takes
a little getting used to.

52
00:02:27,381 --> 00:02:29,016
-We'll be right back.
-Yeah.

53
00:02:31,184 --> 00:02:32,786
-I think I still
see a place where

54
00:02:32,853 --> 00:02:34,488
the pattern's not
matched up, Mike.

55
00:02:34,554 --> 00:02:37,290
Although it is fun when we all
pitch in like this, isn't it?

56
00:02:37,357 --> 00:02:40,394
-Oh! I think the bads are calling.
-Really?

57
00:02:40,460 --> 00:02:42,095
-You know what I found out about you

58
00:02:42,162 --> 00:02:43,764
and your cute little friends, Nelson?

59
00:02:43,830 --> 00:02:45,832
You're part of the counterculture.
That's right!

60
00:02:45,899 --> 00:02:48,435
You're the enemy
of hard working guys like us.

61
00:02:48,769 --> 00:02:53,240
That's why today
is a great day.

62
00:02:53,306 --> 00:02:59,212
For today, we rally in support
of Proposition Deep 13.

63
00:02:59,513 --> 00:03:00,747
Thank you!

64
00:03:00,814 --> 00:03:02,149
Thank you!

65
00:03:02,215 --> 00:03:03,650
Thank you!

66
00:03:03,717 --> 00:03:07,421
Simply put, Proposition Deep 13 means

67
00:03:07,487 --> 00:03:13,093
that we will send you today's experiment,
<i>The Beast of Yucca Flats.</i>

68
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:14,695
Thank you! Thank you!

69
00:03:14,761 --> 00:03:16,697
Thank you! Thank you.

70
00:03:16,763 --> 00:03:20,167
And it will crush
the Satellite of Love, Nelson,

71
00:03:20,233 --> 00:03:23,904
because we've signed a contract
with America.

72
00:03:23,970 --> 00:03:25,872
Tell them what we're
sending them, Frank.

73
00:03:25,939 --> 00:03:28,575
-We're sending
you a wake up call

74
00:03:28,642 --> 00:03:31,745
named<i> The Beast of Yucca Flats.</i>

75
00:03:31,812 --> 00:03:33,847
-Take that, counterculture.

76
00:03:33,914 --> 00:03:37,317
-You're out of touch,
and we hate you.

77
00:03:37,384 --> 00:03:39,286
-Oh, you do, do you?

78
00:03:39,352 --> 00:03:41,388
Well, maybe we can't
match your pomp

79
00:03:41,455 --> 00:03:42,723
and your balloons
and your bunting.

80
00:03:42,789 --> 00:03:44,758
-Oh, hold on, I think I got a horn!

81
00:03:44,825 --> 00:03:48,595
-But what we do have here
is this man, the nation's most

82
00:03:48,662 --> 00:03:50,864
articulate spokesman
for the dream that is

83
00:03:50,931 --> 00:03:52,699
The Satellite of Love.

84
00:03:52,766 --> 00:03:55,235
This fearless man
who has led us,

85
00:03:55,302 --> 00:03:58,739
his quaking sheep, through,
lo, our darkest hours--

86
00:03:58,805 --> 00:04:00,006
-Thank you.
Thank you, Servo.

87
00:04:00,073 --> 00:04:01,875
-[making horn noises]

88
00:04:01,942 --> 00:04:04,978
-Need I describe how he stood
with us before the goons

89
00:04:05,045 --> 00:04:06,813
and truncheons of
Peabody Mining?

90
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:08,882
A loyal friend to the unions!

91
00:04:08,949 --> 00:04:10,550
A--
-Get out of here.

92
00:04:10,617 --> 00:04:11,918
Thank you. Our--
-[making horn noises]

93
00:04:11,985 --> 00:04:13,653
-Thank you.
Our message is a simple one.

94
00:04:13,720 --> 00:04:14,855
-[making horn noises]

95
00:04:14,921 --> 00:04:17,057
-Crow. Crow...

96
00:04:17,124 --> 00:04:19,960
For we have struggled,
yet we have prevailed...

97
00:04:20,026 --> 00:04:22,596
-[making horn noises]
-...through-- please. Thank you.

98
00:04:22,662 --> 00:04:25,298
Through the dark days of<i> Outlaw--</i>
-[making horn noises]

99
00:04:25,365 --> 00:04:27,134
-And-- and<i> The Creeping Terror...</i>

100
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:31,004
[making horn noises] Hey!

101
00:04:31,071 --> 00:04:34,641
-...that we shall triumph,
for we are somebody.

102
00:04:34,708 --> 00:04:35,742
-Yeah!

103
00:04:35,809 --> 00:04:37,244
-Oh, really?

104
00:04:37,310 --> 00:04:40,046
Even if I tell you that
<i>The Beast of Yucca Flats</i>

105
00:04:40,113 --> 00:04:43,884
is another motion picture
from Coleman Francis?

106
00:04:43,950 --> 00:04:46,920
-Well, I think we can survive.

107
00:04:46,987 --> 00:04:48,088
-We're doomed!

108
00:04:48,155 --> 00:04:49,856
-We have been forsaken.

109
00:04:49,923 --> 00:04:51,491
[crying]

110
00:04:51,858 --> 00:04:53,293
-I thought so.

111
00:04:53,360 --> 00:04:56,096
So without further delay,
be prepared

112
00:04:56,163 --> 00:04:58,799
to be crushed by
<i>The Beast of Yucca--</i>

113
00:04:58,865 --> 00:05:01,768
-Actually, uh,
there's a short first.

114
00:05:01,835 --> 00:05:05,739
-After the short,
be prepared to be crushed by--

115
00:05:05,806 --> 00:05:08,608
-Oh, uh, actually there's
another short after that.

116
00:05:08,675 --> 00:05:11,011
-In about a half an hour.
Would that work?

117
00:05:11,077 --> 00:05:16,049
Be prepared to be crushed
by<i> The Beast of Yucca Flats!</i>

118
00:05:16,116 --> 00:05:17,884
-Come on.
We can do it, guys.

119
00:05:17,951 --> 00:05:20,153
The dream will never die.

120
00:05:21,054 --> 00:05:22,489
-[crying]
-[movie sign buzzer]

121
00:05:22,556 --> 00:05:23,690
-Oh, we got Movie Sign!

122
00:05:23,757 --> 00:05:25,559
-Oh, we got Movie Sign!

123
00:05:36,937 --> 00:05:38,438
[music playing]

124
00:05:38,505 --> 00:05:39,606
-[Crow and Servo crying]

125
00:05:39,673 --> 00:05:40,807
-[Mike] Oh, come on.
It's gut check time.

126
00:05:40,874 --> 00:05:41,875
We can do it.

127
00:05:41,942 --> 00:05:44,778
-[Crow] And talks and talks.
I can't get a word in edgewise.

128
00:05:45,912 --> 00:05:48,415
-[Servo] Ah, for high school
students from 7 to 70.

129
00:05:48,481 --> 00:05:50,450
-[Mike] I'll bet high school
students flocked

130
00:05:50,517 --> 00:05:52,118
to this movie in droves.

131
00:05:55,722 --> 00:05:57,357
-Produced by imps!

132
00:05:57,757 --> 00:06:00,694
-[Servo] Promoting dullardism throughout
this great land of ours.

133
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:01,928
[music playing]

134
00:06:02,028 --> 00:06:03,597
[Crow and Servo humming with music]

135
00:06:04,598 --> 00:06:07,033
-[Servo] Money is fun.
-[laughter]

136
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:08,168
-[Crow] When it's fun.

137
00:06:09,970 --> 00:06:12,372
-[Crow] OK, so we steal
the money tonight.

138
00:06:13,907 --> 00:06:15,909
-[Servo] Ooh, he's had his
chest excavated.

139
00:06:21,248 --> 00:06:23,850
-[Mike] Greasy scarecrow boy not
invited.

140
00:06:25,719 --> 00:06:27,787
-Want to go to the dance with me?

141
00:06:29,689 --> 00:06:32,225
-[boy, voiceover]<i> Well, here we go again.</i>

142
00:06:32,893 --> 00:06:36,930
<i>Why is it that I always run out of money</i>
<i>just when I need it?</i>

143
00:06:36,997 --> 00:06:38,098
-[Servo] Why do I caulk my hair?

144
00:06:38,164 --> 00:06:39,666
-[boy]<i> Why am I always broke?</i>

145
00:06:39,733 --> 00:06:41,701
-Maybe it's my crack habit.

146
00:06:41,768 --> 00:06:45,772
-[boy]<i> Bob buys himself a car,</i>
<i>while I have to worry about</i>

147
00:06:45,839 --> 00:06:48,275
<i>wearing a hat</i>
<i>to take his sister to a dance.</i>

148
00:06:48,341 --> 00:06:49,643
-[Crow] Maybe it's these
mortgage payments.

149
00:06:49,709 --> 00:06:51,811
Should have bought
a smaller house.

150
00:06:52,279 --> 00:06:53,513
-[boy]<i> I don't get it.</i>

151
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:54,848
-[Servo] Ah, the appeal of Adam Sandler.

152
00:06:54,915 --> 00:06:58,351
-[boy]<i> Other people don't seem to worry</i>
<i>about money all the time.</i>

153
00:06:59,152 --> 00:07:00,987
<i>Why am I always in the red?</i>

154
00:07:03,189 --> 00:07:05,592
-[Crow] Even having a huge
Mallomar doesn't help.

155
00:07:05,659 --> 00:07:06,826
-[boy]<i> $0.50.</i>

156
00:07:07,861 --> 00:07:08,962
<i>Half a dollar.</i>

157
00:07:09,029 --> 00:07:10,964
-[Servo] In those days,
that'd buy you a car.

158
00:07:11,865 --> 00:07:13,233
-[boy]<i> Benjamin Franklin, eh?</i>

159
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:14,301
-Bite me, Franklin.

160
00:07:14,367 --> 00:07:17,504
<i>-[boy] He was supposed to be a pretty</i>
<i>smart fellow when it came to money.</i>

161
00:07:18,638 --> 00:07:21,174
<i>I suppose he could have told me</i>
<i>how to keep out of the red.</i>

162
00:07:21,241 --> 00:07:23,310
-He was the best
president we ever had.

163
00:07:23,376 --> 00:07:25,011
-[boy]<i> Aw, what's the use?</i>

164
00:07:27,213 --> 00:07:29,683
-[Servo] Sorcerer!
-[shivers]

165
00:07:29,849 --> 00:07:32,619
-[Mike] Why must I be a young
Christopher Walken?

166
00:07:33,687 --> 00:07:35,355
-There's no place like a bank.

167
00:07:35,422 --> 00:07:37,123
There's no place like a bank.

168
00:07:40,060 --> 00:07:41,361
-[Servo] Alfred Hitchcock!

169
00:07:41,428 --> 00:07:42,629
-[man] William.

170
00:07:42,696 --> 00:07:43,863
William, my boy.

171
00:07:43,930 --> 00:07:45,565
-[Mike] Can you borrow me $1.00?

172
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,068
-Who's that?

173
00:07:48,568 --> 00:07:49,669
-[man] Over here, son.

174
00:07:49,736 --> 00:07:51,638
-[Crow] Directly in front of you.

175
00:07:51,705 --> 00:07:53,606
-[Servo] Tom Bosley's come to haunt him.

176
00:07:54,074 --> 00:07:55,976
-Don't you recognize me?

177
00:07:56,309 --> 00:07:58,979
You were just looking at
me on that half dollar.

178
00:07:59,379 --> 00:08:01,281
-[Mike] Just reach for the gun.

179
00:08:02,349 --> 00:08:04,384
-Are you Benjamin Franklin?

180
00:08:04,451 --> 00:08:06,619
-[Benjamin] That's right.
Come over here, son.

181
00:08:06,686 --> 00:08:07,854
I'd like to talk to you.

182
00:08:07,921 --> 00:08:10,090
-Could you have your slave press my suit?

183
00:08:11,958 --> 00:08:14,127
-[Benjamin] That's right. Over here.

184
00:08:14,394 --> 00:08:16,262
-[Servo laughs]
-Thank you, boy.

185
00:08:17,130 --> 00:08:19,299
Perhaps I can be
of some help to you

186
00:08:19,366 --> 00:08:22,869
in removing you from what you
so quaintly describe as "the red".

187
00:08:22,936 --> 00:08:24,904
-[Servo] For a price!
-What do you mean?

188
00:08:24,971 --> 00:08:26,840
-Now, I don't think
that your problem

189
00:08:26,906 --> 00:08:28,708
is a difficult one, William.

190
00:08:28,775 --> 00:08:31,544
You have one important
thing to learn,

191
00:08:31,611 --> 00:08:33,646
and then you'll have no problem.

192
00:08:33,713 --> 00:08:36,316
You must learn how
to manage your money.

193
00:08:37,183 --> 00:08:38,251
-What money?

194
00:08:38,318 --> 00:08:40,487
-[Servo] Don't smart-mouth me, boy.

195
00:08:40,553 --> 00:08:41,955
-You've a point there, son.

196
00:08:42,022 --> 00:08:44,424
You must have some money
before you can manage it.

197
00:08:44,758 --> 00:08:46,993
But you do not fare
badly on that score.

198
00:08:47,060 --> 00:08:49,095
-[Servo] You can sell your body!
-I don't?

199
00:08:49,529 --> 00:08:50,930
-No.

200
00:08:51,131 --> 00:08:52,232
Let me show you.

201
00:08:52,298 --> 00:08:55,769
-[Crow] Will our mystery guest
enter and sign in, please?

202
00:08:56,870 --> 00:08:59,606
-[Benjamin] Come on, boy.
-[Mike] Jump on my stomach.

203
00:08:59,806 --> 00:09:01,708
-Come a little closer.

204
00:09:01,775 --> 00:09:06,780
Suppose you look in this mirror
and see what it tells you.

205
00:09:06,846 --> 00:09:07,847
-[Mike] God, I'm fat.

206
00:09:07,914 --> 00:09:12,819
-[Benjamin] Now, take a good look
at you and your earnings.

207
00:09:13,219 --> 00:09:14,554
-[Crow] I'm Kate Moss!

208
00:09:14,621 --> 00:09:18,191
-[Benjamin]<i> You receive $2.00 every week</i>
<i>as an allowance from your father.</i>

209
00:09:18,258 --> 00:09:19,259
-[Mike] James Joyce.

210
00:09:19,325 --> 00:09:21,361
-[Benjamin]<i> Something quite unheard of</i>
<i>in my day.</i>

211
00:09:21,428 --> 00:09:22,562
-Fathers?

212
00:09:23,029 --> 00:09:27,333
<i>-[Benjamin] Then, you earn about $3.00</i>
<i>each week delivering for the pharmacy.</i>

213
00:09:27,400 --> 00:09:28,401
-[Servo] Here's your sherry.

214
00:09:28,468 --> 00:09:30,804
-[Benjamin]<i> In addition,</i>
<i>you obtain another dollar or so</i>

215
00:09:30,870 --> 00:09:32,338
<i>in what you call "tips".</i>

216
00:09:32,405 --> 00:09:33,807
-Or protection.

217
00:09:34,307 --> 00:09:37,010
-[Benjamin] You earn between $6.00
and $7.00 a week, William.

218
00:09:37,077 --> 00:09:38,311
-[Mike, Servo and Crow] Ooh.

219
00:09:38,378 --> 00:09:41,381
-A tidy sum for a boy
who's still in school.

220
00:09:41,448 --> 00:09:45,351
-Maybe so, but I never have
any money when I need it.

221
00:09:45,618 --> 00:09:48,188
-That's not because you're
a poor earner, William.

222
00:09:48,354 --> 00:09:50,123
It's because you're
a poor spender.

223
00:09:50,190 --> 00:09:51,391
-[Crow] And a bad person.

224
00:09:51,458 --> 00:09:52,859
-Poor spender?

225
00:09:53,293 --> 00:09:54,327
-Yes, my boy.

226
00:09:54,394 --> 00:09:56,529
You spend your money
without any plan.

227
00:09:57,931 --> 00:09:59,632
You buy whenever the mood strikes you.

228
00:09:59,699 --> 00:10:01,534
-[Servo] Furs, cars, trips to Vegas.

229
00:10:01,601 --> 00:10:03,937
-[Benjamin] You spend your money as
fast as you acquire it.

230
00:10:04,003 --> 00:10:05,939
-[Servo] Ugh.
-[Benjamin] With no thought

231
00:10:06,005 --> 00:10:07,707
for the future.
-[Servo] You idiot.

232
00:10:07,774 --> 00:10:10,009
-[Benjamin]<i> Then,</i>
<i>when something comes along</i>

233
00:10:10,076 --> 00:10:12,879
<i>where you need money,</i>
<i>you do not have it.</i>

234
00:10:12,946 --> 00:10:15,515
-On behalf of all girls, none of us
are going to the dance with you.

235
00:10:15,582 --> 00:10:16,950
-I guess you're right.

236
00:10:17,050 --> 00:10:18,485
But what should I do about it?

237
00:10:18,551 --> 00:10:19,552
-[Servo] I don't know!

238
00:10:19,619 --> 00:10:21,688
-Whatever you want to do, my boy.

239
00:10:21,754 --> 00:10:25,258
The important thing
is to want to do something.

240
00:10:25,692 --> 00:10:28,461
Now, judging from these drawings,

241
00:10:29,262 --> 00:10:31,064
-you'd like to be an architect someday.

242
00:10:31,131 --> 00:10:32,398
-[Servo] Forget it, soda jerk.
-That's right.

243
00:10:32,465 --> 00:10:35,468
-Fine.
Then let me ask you a question.

244
00:10:35,935 --> 00:10:38,805
Why should anyone pay an architect
for house plans?

245
00:10:38,872 --> 00:10:41,407
Why shouldn't he just go
right ahead and build it?

246
00:10:42,375 --> 00:10:43,843
-He wouldn't know
what to do first.

247
00:10:43,910 --> 00:10:45,211
He wouldn't have a plan.

248
00:10:45,378 --> 00:10:46,379
-[Benjamin] Exactly.

249
00:10:47,080 --> 00:10:49,048
The plan is a guide.

250
00:10:49,115 --> 00:10:50,950
A means to an end.

251
00:10:51,017 --> 00:10:55,388
It enables a man
to build the kind of house he wants

252
00:10:55,455 --> 00:10:57,056
for a price he can afford to pay.

253
00:10:57,123 --> 00:10:58,124
-[Servo] Ow, my gout!
Oh.

254
00:10:58,191 --> 00:11:01,995
-And the same thing, William,
holds true in handling money.

255
00:11:02,061 --> 00:11:03,062
-[Servo] I hate you.

256
00:11:03,129 --> 00:11:06,032
-You must decide what you
want your money to do for you

257
00:11:06,099 --> 00:11:09,135
and then, plan your
spending accordingly.

258
00:11:09,602 --> 00:11:11,571
You've heard this before.

259
00:11:11,638 --> 00:11:13,806
Plan your spending.
-[Mike] Ding!

260
00:11:13,873 --> 00:11:16,976
-[William] Oh, that sounds all right
if you have a lot of money to plan with.

261
00:11:17,043 --> 00:11:18,077
-[Servo] Listen, little toad.

262
00:11:18,144 --> 00:11:20,580
-Believe me, William,
it's even more important

263
00:11:20,647 --> 00:11:22,882
if you do not have
a great deal of money.

264
00:11:22,949 --> 00:11:24,250
-How come?

265
00:11:24,317 --> 00:11:26,452
-Because the less
money you have,

266
00:11:26,519 --> 00:11:28,688
the more careful
you must be with it.

267
00:11:29,389 --> 00:11:30,890
Did you ever hear of a budget, William?

268
00:11:30,957 --> 00:11:32,225
-Uh, that's a fish, right?

269
00:11:32,292 --> 00:11:33,893
-I sure did.

270
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:35,028
Just last night.

271
00:11:35,094 --> 00:11:37,497
-[Servo] Whoa. I don't think I want
to hear about this.

272
00:11:38,398 --> 00:11:40,266
-[man] I'm sorry, Bill.
-[Mike] I'll have to break your legs.

273
00:11:40,333 --> 00:11:42,068
-You have to learn
to manage your money.

274
00:11:42,135 --> 00:11:44,704
I can't let you go along thinking
that money grows on trees.

275
00:11:44,771 --> 00:11:47,507
-But I only need another
dollar and a half, Dad.

276
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:50,343
-You'd have the money for that
dance if you kept a budget.

277
00:11:50,410 --> 00:11:51,844
I have to keep one, you know.

278
00:11:52,912 --> 00:11:54,347
-[Servo] You talking to me?

279
00:11:54,414 --> 00:11:56,115
-[Benjamin] Your father was right,
William.

280
00:11:56,549 --> 00:11:58,618
He has to keep a budget to be sure

281
00:11:58,685 --> 00:12:01,287
that all essential items
are taken care of.

282
00:12:01,354 --> 00:12:03,690
-[Servo] Dope.
-And it's the same for you.

283
00:12:03,756 --> 00:12:05,325
-Me? Oh, no.

284
00:12:05,391 --> 00:12:07,694
That budgeting business
sounds too complicated.

285
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:10,096
-But your budget
needn't be complicated.

286
00:12:10,163 --> 00:12:12,899
You haven't as many things
to consider as your father has.

287
00:12:12,966 --> 00:12:16,302
Here. This is the idea.
-[Mike] Ding!

288
00:12:16,369 --> 00:12:18,071
-[Benjamin] A budget should fit the user.

289
00:12:18,137 --> 00:12:19,172
-Like a teddy.

290
00:12:19,239 --> 00:12:21,541
-[Benjamin] It is designed
to fit the person

291
00:12:21,608 --> 00:12:23,810
who's using it.
-[Mike] Jay Gatsby.

292
00:12:23,876 --> 00:12:26,079
-[Benjamin]<i> Take the budget</i>
<i>of your friend Bob, for instance.</i>

293
00:12:26,145 --> 00:12:27,981
-[Servo] Bob's a better
person than you are.

294
00:12:28,047 --> 00:12:30,750
-[Benjamin]<i> He keeps a record</i>
<i>of each expenditure.</i>

295
00:12:30,817 --> 00:12:31,985
-Bob is anal.

296
00:12:33,419 --> 00:12:37,090
-[Benjamin]<i> Then, all he has to do</i>
<i>is look at his budget [audio skipping]--</i>

297
00:12:37,156 --> 00:12:39,325
<i>exactly how he stands.</i>

298
00:12:39,392 --> 00:12:40,393
-[Servo] Hm.

299
00:12:40,460 --> 00:12:43,062
-[Benjamin]<i> Notice that last item</i>
<i>on his budget, William?</i>

300
00:12:43,630 --> 00:12:44,831
<i>Savings?</i>

301
00:12:44,897 --> 00:12:46,399
-[Servo] Get it, William?

302
00:12:46,466 --> 00:12:48,835
-How does he ever
have that much left?

303
00:12:48,901 --> 00:12:50,870
-He doesn't have it "left", William.

304
00:12:51,170 --> 00:12:53,706
If Bob saved only what he had left,

305
00:12:53,773 --> 00:12:55,408
he would not have that car.

306
00:12:55,475 --> 00:12:57,910
-Huh?
-He sets that aside first.

307
00:12:57,977 --> 00:13:01,247
-[Mike] Uh.
-Boy, what willpower.

308
00:13:01,314 --> 00:13:03,249
-[Benjamin] No, it isn't just willpower.

309
00:13:03,316 --> 00:13:05,985
You see, Bob had a goal.

310
00:13:06,052 --> 00:13:07,053
-[Servo] And two assists!

311
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:08,888
-He knew two years ago
that he wanted a car

312
00:13:08,955 --> 00:13:12,525
when he finished high school,
so he began saving for it.

313
00:13:13,559 --> 00:13:16,262
<i>Each week, he went down to the bank...</i>
-[Crow] And robbed it!

314
00:13:16,329 --> 00:13:18,331
<i>-...and deposited his savings regularly.</i>

315
00:13:18,798 --> 00:13:20,867
<i>And the extra money he made</i>
<i>during vacations</i>

316
00:13:20,933 --> 00:13:23,336
<i>helped his bank account grow, too.</i>

317
00:13:23,403 --> 00:13:26,739
<i>Until one day, he had enough</i>
<i>to buy the car he wanted.</i>

318
00:13:26,806 --> 00:13:28,174
-[Mike] But then he crashed
his car and died.

319
00:13:28,241 --> 00:13:30,943
-[Benjamin]<i> Bob remembered</i>
<i>one important rule.</i>

320
00:13:31,010 --> 00:13:32,312
-[Servo] Stuffing instead of potatoes.

321
00:13:32,378 --> 00:13:35,348
-[Benjamin]<i> Save for a goal,</i>

322
00:13:35,415 --> 00:13:38,084
<i>so you can buy something</i>
<i>you really want later on.</i>

323
00:13:38,751 --> 00:13:41,988
You see, William, it's just as true today

324
00:13:42,055 --> 00:13:44,324
as it was the first time I said it.

325
00:13:44,490 --> 00:13:47,260
"A penny saved is a penny earned."

326
00:13:47,327 --> 00:13:48,761
-Thank you. I'm out of here.

327
00:13:48,828 --> 00:13:52,131
-Now, suppose you
decided to have a budget.

328
00:13:52,198 --> 00:13:53,633
How would you start?

329
00:13:53,733 --> 00:13:55,835
-Well...
-[Mike] With lots of money.

330
00:13:55,902 --> 00:13:58,805
-The first thing I would do
is to estimate my income.

331
00:13:59,539 --> 00:14:01,674
-That's right. Figure it out.

332
00:14:01,741 --> 00:14:03,109
By the week, in your case.

333
00:14:03,176 --> 00:14:05,011
-[Servo] Shouldn't take long
to estimate $0.50.

334
00:14:05,078 --> 00:14:09,082
-Estimate my income.

335
00:14:09,148 --> 00:14:10,917
-[Benjamin] Then you must decide just how

336
00:14:11,017 --> 00:14:12,952
you're going to spend your money.

337
00:14:13,019 --> 00:14:14,053
-That's right.

338
00:14:14,120 --> 00:14:15,555
Plan my spending.

339
00:14:16,222 --> 00:14:20,093
Plan my spending.

340
00:14:20,159 --> 00:14:22,095
-Could you get your stomach
off my desk, please?

341
00:14:22,161 --> 00:14:23,162
-How do I do that?

342
00:14:23,229 --> 00:14:24,497
-Well...

343
00:14:24,564 --> 00:14:27,166
-[Mike] Have illegitimate children.
-[robots laugh]

344
00:14:27,233 --> 00:14:30,236
-You may not be able to do
it very accurately at first,

345
00:14:30,303 --> 00:14:31,871
but you'll keep
adjusting your budget

346
00:14:31,938 --> 00:14:35,508
as you go along until you get it,
so that it fits.

347
00:14:35,575 --> 00:14:37,210
And that's the third point.

348
00:14:37,276 --> 00:14:38,745
Keep it flexible.

349
00:14:39,212 --> 00:14:41,347
-Keep...
-[Mike] Pretending to write.

350
00:14:41,414 --> 00:14:43,816
-...budget flexible.

351
00:14:43,883 --> 00:14:47,120
-[Benjamin] Yes.
Do not ever let it become too rigid.

352
00:14:47,186 --> 00:14:49,455
If you do,
you'll become a prisoner to it.

353
00:14:49,522 --> 00:14:50,523
-[Mike] Hey!

354
00:14:50,590 --> 00:14:54,227
-And finally, you must
plan your savings.

355
00:14:54,293 --> 00:14:57,897
-[William] Plan my savings.

356
00:14:58,564 --> 00:14:59,932
-[Servo] William,
what are you doing in there?

357
00:14:59,999 --> 00:15:02,068
You're not talking
to the founding fathers again, are you?

358
00:15:02,135 --> 00:15:05,338
-If you'll remember these
few simple rules, William,

359
00:15:05,438 --> 00:15:10,977
I'm sure that you'll find it
very easy to stay out of "the red".

360
00:15:11,043 --> 00:15:13,646
-Dead people have
too much time on their hands.

361
00:15:13,713 --> 00:15:16,015
-[Servo] Oh, and, uh,
kill your parents. Bye.

362
00:15:17,784 --> 00:15:19,285
-[Mike] But it was too late.

363
00:15:19,352 --> 00:15:21,454
William filed Chapter 11.

364
00:15:23,456 --> 00:15:25,725
-That was my darkest vision yet.

365
00:15:25,992 --> 00:15:27,994
[Servo humming eerie music]

366
00:15:30,730 --> 00:15:33,433
-[Servo] I knew then,
I had to blow up the school.

367
00:15:36,936 --> 00:15:37,970
-[Servo] Ooh.

368
00:15:38,037 --> 00:15:39,806
-[Mike] Son, you're in deep
to mother and me.

369
00:15:39,872 --> 00:15:41,207
-That's interesting, Bill.

370
00:15:41,641 --> 00:15:42,775
-Oh, hi, Dad.

371
00:15:43,443 --> 00:15:44,577
-A budget, isn't it?

372
00:15:44,644 --> 00:15:45,945
-Yep.
Just finishing it.

373
00:15:46,012 --> 00:15:48,948
-Yeah, this is my weekly
feigning of interest in you, son.

374
00:15:50,483 --> 00:15:52,118
-[Servo] You're gonna buy a dress?

375
00:15:52,185 --> 00:15:54,253
-Hm. Pretty good.

376
00:15:54,320 --> 00:15:55,388
-Helter Skelter.
Very nice.

377
00:15:55,455 --> 00:15:57,123
-How'd you happen to do this?

378
00:15:57,790 --> 00:16:00,326
-Oh, I just got to thinking.

379
00:16:00,393 --> 00:16:03,296
-I see.
Well, it'll be interesting to see

380
00:16:03,362 --> 00:16:05,465
how long you stick to it.
-[Mike] You little loser.

381
00:16:05,531 --> 00:16:08,634
-Oh, I don't intend to stick
too close to this to start with.

382
00:16:08,701 --> 00:16:11,838
I'll keep adjusting it as I go along.
-Oh?

383
00:16:11,904 --> 00:16:14,874
-Sure. That's important,
to keep a budget flexible.

384
00:16:14,941 --> 00:16:18,044
-Like Gumby.
-Or you'll find yourself a prisoner to it.

385
00:16:18,110 --> 00:16:20,613
-[Servo] By the way, have you seen
your mother lately?

386
00:16:20,680 --> 00:16:22,248
-You got a point there, son.

387
00:16:22,648 --> 00:16:25,284
-[Mike] Well, I've got to go sit
downstairs.

388
00:16:26,252 --> 00:16:28,254
-Take that budget of yours, Dad.

389
00:16:28,321 --> 00:16:29,322
-And shove it.

390
00:16:29,388 --> 00:16:32,258
-Don't you think maybe
it's a little too rigid?

391
00:16:32,592 --> 00:16:34,594
-What?
-[Servo laughing]

392
00:16:34,660 --> 00:16:36,929
-Couldn't you stretch it out
this one time?

393
00:16:36,996 --> 00:16:39,599
So you could let me have a dollar
and a half for that dance?

394
00:16:39,665 --> 00:16:40,967
-[Mike] All right, son.

395
00:16:41,033 --> 00:16:43,135
I will go to the dance with you.

396
00:16:43,803 --> 00:16:45,805
-OK, son, you win this time.

397
00:16:46,706 --> 00:16:50,610
Oh, uh, what'll I charge
this to on my budget?

398
00:16:50,910 --> 00:16:52,044
-Education.
-Whose?

399
00:16:52,111 --> 00:16:53,379
Yours or mine?

400
00:16:53,446 --> 00:16:55,515
-See you next week, son.

401
00:16:55,581 --> 00:16:56,849
-Here you are, son.

402
00:16:56,916 --> 00:16:59,318
-Gee, thanks, Dad.
-Thanks a lot, loser.

403
00:17:01,654 --> 00:17:02,855
-[William] Thank you, Mr. Franklin.

404
00:17:02,922 --> 00:17:05,258
-[Servo] For making us laugh
about love again.

405
00:17:05,324 --> 00:17:08,694
-[Mike] Benjamin Franklin was tried in
the 8th Circuit Court on stalking charges.

406
00:17:08,761 --> 00:17:10,463
In a minute, the results of that trial.

407
00:17:13,266 --> 00:17:15,167
[all exclaiming]

408
00:17:15,234 --> 00:17:16,869
-[Crow] I'm having a freak-out
up to 10 years later!

409
00:17:18,604 --> 00:17:20,039
[narrator]<i> An American democracy.</i>

410
00:17:20,106 --> 00:17:21,374
-[Servo] Would be really great.

411
00:17:21,440 --> 00:17:24,410
[narrator]<i> Island of old world elegance.</i>
-Guam?

412
00:17:24,477 --> 00:17:26,679
[narrator]<i> Rich in its heritage</i>
<i>of art and culture.</i>

413
00:17:26,746 --> 00:17:29,015
-[Mike] With wah-wah pedals
playing constantly.

414
00:17:29,081 --> 00:17:31,551
[narrator]<i> A wealth</i>
<i>of historic grandeur and beauty.</i>

415
00:17:31,617 --> 00:17:32,718
-[Servo] Does not exist here.

416
00:17:35,054 --> 00:17:37,990
-[Mike] Who's the cat who would risk
his life for a fellow man?

417
00:17:38,224 --> 00:17:40,793
Shaft.
Yeah. Can you dig it?

418
00:17:40,860 --> 00:17:44,130
[narrator]<i> Year round sun</i>
<i>makes this island a vacation paradise.</i>

419
00:17:44,196 --> 00:17:46,165
-[Crow] And very hard to sleep.

420
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:50,570
[narrator]<i> And today,</i>
<i>it is also a land of progress.</i>

421
00:17:50,636 --> 00:17:52,572
-[Servo] But we can't tell you
where it is.

422
00:17:52,872 --> 00:17:55,241
[narrator]<i> Building into the clean</i>
<i>blue skies,</i>

423
00:17:55,308 --> 00:17:57,410
<i>-the island is on the move.</i>
-[Mike] Hawaii?

424
00:17:57,476 --> 00:17:59,845
-No, an island.
-[narrator]<i> Apartment complexes,</i>

425
00:18:00,279 --> 00:18:01,747
<i>bilingual schools...</i>

426
00:18:01,814 --> 00:18:03,249
-[Servo] Bisexual students.

427
00:18:03,482 --> 00:18:05,351
[narrator]<i> ...modern hospitals...</i>
-[Servo] Are not here.

428
00:18:05,418 --> 00:18:07,887
[narrator]<i> ...luxury hotels.</i>
-Are desperately needed.

429
00:18:08,354 --> 00:18:10,323
[narrator]<i> Progress can be seen</i>
<i>everywhere.</i>

430
00:18:10,389 --> 00:18:12,158
-[Mike] In places other than this.

431
00:18:12,224 --> 00:18:14,660
-Circle Pines is really thriving.

432
00:18:14,994 --> 00:18:19,632
[narrator]<i> This is Puerto Rico,</i>
<i>Progress Island, USA.</i>

433
00:18:19,699 --> 00:18:22,001
-Oh. When did they change the name?

434
00:18:22,802 --> 00:18:24,971
-[Servo] A Quinn Martin production.

435
00:18:25,838 --> 00:18:30,076
-[all humming along with the music]

436
00:18:30,142 --> 00:18:31,477
-[all] Puerto Rico!

437
00:18:31,877 --> 00:18:35,414
[narrator]<i> Puerto Rico,</i>
<i>a 1,000 miles southeast of Florida,</i>

438
00:18:35,481 --> 00:18:38,551
<i>where the Atlantic meets the Caribbean.</i>
-[Mike] And they really hit it off.

439
00:18:38,618 --> 00:18:40,386
[narrator]<i> A land the size</i>
<i>of Rhode Island,</i>

440
00:18:40,453 --> 00:18:42,788
<i>it is just as American in its way of life.</i>

441
00:18:42,855 --> 00:18:45,057
-So you might as well
just stay where you are.

442
00:18:45,725 --> 00:18:47,360
-[Servo] Ah, indigenous cuisine.

443
00:18:48,694 --> 00:18:50,429
-[Mike] With this, and this, and that!

444
00:18:52,031 --> 00:18:54,266
-[Crow] Here, money is
exchanged for coconuts.

445
00:18:54,634 --> 00:18:57,903
[narrator]<i> Puerto Rico is permanently</i>
<i>associated with the United States.</i>

446
00:18:57,970 --> 00:19:00,740
<i>Its three million people are US citizens,</i>

447
00:19:00,806 --> 00:19:04,410
<i>and like other Americans,</i>
<i>enjoy a representative form of government.</i>

448
00:19:04,477 --> 00:19:06,479
-[Servo] Feel the glory
of the royal scam.

449
00:19:06,812 --> 00:19:08,681
[narrator]<i> The capital, in San Juan,</i>

450
00:19:08,748 --> 00:19:10,783
<i>where the Senate and</i>
<i>House of Representatives</i>

451
00:19:10,850 --> 00:19:12,118
<i>form the legislature.</i>

452
00:19:12,184 --> 00:19:14,086
-[Mike] Newt Gingrich
is the head of that, too.

453
00:19:14,153 --> 00:19:17,957
[narrator]<i> La Fortaleza,</i>
<i>built by the Spanish in 1540,</i>

454
00:19:18,024 --> 00:19:20,526
<i>it is the oldest governor's mansion</i>
<i>in the Western Hemisphere.</i>

455
00:19:20,593 --> 00:19:22,895
-And it shows. Whoo!

456
00:19:25,698 --> 00:19:29,502
[narrator]<i> The promise of this land</i>
<i>is fostered through education.</i>

457
00:19:29,568 --> 00:19:32,271
<i>Institutions like</i>
<i>the University of Puerto Rico</i>

458
00:19:32,338 --> 00:19:34,540
<i>contribute to continuing progress.</i>

459
00:19:34,607 --> 00:19:35,775
-[Servo] But not much.

460
00:19:36,575 --> 00:19:40,646
[narrator]<i> Here, one-third of</i>
<i>the entire population is in schools.</i>

461
00:19:40,713 --> 00:19:42,748
-[Mike] Latka Gravas goes to class.

462
00:19:42,815 --> 00:19:44,550
-[Servo imitates Latka]
Thank you very much.

463
00:19:44,617 --> 00:19:46,919
-It's room<i> dos dos dos.</i>

464
00:19:47,053 --> 00:19:49,622
[narrator]<i> Today,</i>
<i>the island's educational system</i>

465
00:19:49,689 --> 00:19:51,957
<i>attracts young people</i>
<i>from around the world.</i>

466
00:19:52,024 --> 00:19:54,093
-[Servo] But mainly from Puerto Rico.

467
00:19:56,328 --> 00:19:58,864
-[Mike] Here, we're flying in
another trumpet section.

468
00:19:59,265 --> 00:20:01,100
-That nose wheel feels mushy.

469
00:20:01,967 --> 00:20:04,270
[narrator]<i> San Juan International</i>
<i>Airport,</i>

470
00:20:04,336 --> 00:20:07,373
<i>crossroads of a global network</i>
<i>of air transportation.</i>

471
00:20:07,440 --> 00:20:09,942
-[Servo] It's real neat. Ooh!

472
00:20:12,011 --> 00:20:14,747
[narrator]<i> Travel from anywhere</i>
<i>in the world in a matter of hours.</i>

473
00:20:14,814 --> 00:20:17,149
-[Mike] Just like everywhere
else in the world.

474
00:20:18,084 --> 00:20:20,219
-OK, let's get funky now.

475
00:20:21,854 --> 00:20:24,323
-[Servo] Yes, even the air
supports airplanes

476
00:20:24,390 --> 00:20:26,425
just as it does
in the not better United States.

477
00:20:26,492 --> 00:20:28,227
[narrator]<i> For the visitor,</i>
<i>Progress Island</i>

478
00:20:28,294 --> 00:20:31,030
<i>offers a tremendous</i>
<i>variety of experiences,</i>

479
00:20:31,097 --> 00:20:33,799
<i>beginning with the rich and</i>
<i>colorful heritage of the Caribbean.</i>

480
00:20:33,866 --> 00:20:36,736
-Which we buried
in order to build skyscrapers.

481
00:20:38,370 --> 00:20:40,039
-[Mike] Look, just come here.

482
00:20:40,573 --> 00:20:42,942
{\an8}[narrator]<i> The fortress</i>
<i>of San Felipe del Morro.</i>

483
00:20:43,008 --> 00:20:45,911
-[Servo] Is to be avoided at all times.
-[Crow] Ooh.

484
00:20:45,978 --> 00:20:48,114
[narrator]<i> Constructed in 1591,</i>

485
00:20:48,180 --> 00:20:51,117
<i>it withstood the attacks</i>
<i>of Sir Francis Drake,</i>

486
00:20:51,183 --> 00:20:54,086
<i>and witnessed many battles</i>
<i>throughout its proud history.</i>

487
00:20:54,787 --> 00:20:56,922
-[Servo] Fun times at Guantanamo.

488
00:20:59,325 --> 00:21:00,493
-[Servo, Mike and Crow] Progress!

489
00:21:00,860 --> 00:21:02,895
[narrator]<i> The streets</i>
<i>of old San Juan,</i>

490
00:21:02,962 --> 00:21:05,865
<i>once the cornerstone of</i>
<i>Spain's Caribbean empire.</i>

491
00:21:05,931 --> 00:21:08,534
-Now the site of numerous muggings.
-[Servo] No!

492
00:21:09,769 --> 00:21:14,874
<i>♪ Parsley, sage, rosemary and thyme ♪</i>

493
00:21:15,674 --> 00:21:18,644
[narrator]<i> Casablanca,</i>
<i>home of Ponce de Leon,</i>

494
00:21:18,711 --> 00:21:20,579
<i>who sailed with</i>
<i>Columbus and established</i>

495
00:21:20,646 --> 00:21:22,581
<i>the first permanent</i>
<i>settlement on the island.</i>

496
00:21:22,648 --> 00:21:24,683
-[Servo] Oh, thanks a lot, Ponce.

497
00:21:25,217 --> 00:21:27,586
-And here's where the nuns sleep.

498
00:21:28,921 --> 00:21:31,791
[narrator]<i> In the rooms and</i>
<i>courtyards of Casablanca,</i>

499
00:21:31,857 --> 00:21:34,994
<i>one can still hear the echoes</i>
<i>of the Spanish main.</i>

500
00:21:35,060 --> 00:21:36,529
-[all] Whoa!

501
00:21:36,595 --> 00:21:39,532
[narrator] ...authentic<i> costume, dance,</i>

502
00:21:39,598 --> 00:21:41,934
<i>and music of old San Juan.</i>
-[Crow] Are not here.

503
00:21:46,172 --> 00:21:49,008
-[Crow] Valerie Harper lookalike
contests are held.

504
00:21:49,742 --> 00:21:53,479
-[Mike] Yes, no matter what the culture,
folk dancing is stupid.

505
00:21:54,713 --> 00:21:56,982
-[Servo] Here, Up With People get down.

506
00:21:58,150 --> 00:22:02,488
<i>-[Mike] ♪ Don't break my heart</i>
<i>My achy breaky heart Puerto Rican heart ♪</i>

507
00:22:02,555 --> 00:22:05,324
-Well, thanks for coming out
tonight and supporting live folk dancing.

508
00:22:05,391 --> 00:22:07,226
We have another show coming up, so don't--

509
00:22:07,726 --> 00:22:09,862
[narrator]<i> The Museum of Art in Ponce,</i>

510
00:22:09,929 --> 00:22:12,131
<i>a perfect setting for a family outing.</i>

511
00:22:12,198 --> 00:22:14,133
-[Crow] I wish we had that for you.

512
00:22:14,200 --> 00:22:15,901
[Mike laughs]

513
00:22:17,403 --> 00:22:20,139
[narrator]<i> The virtuoso Pablo Casals...</i>
-[Crow] Is porky.

514
00:22:20,206 --> 00:22:22,641
[narrator]<i> ...founder of the island's</i>
<i>finest music festival.</i>

515
00:22:22,708 --> 00:22:25,311
<i>The museum collection</i>
<i>represents five centuries</i>

516
00:22:25,377 --> 00:22:27,213
<i>of American and European art.</i>

517
00:22:27,279 --> 00:22:28,948
-[Mike] I'm not speaking to you.

518
00:22:29,181 --> 00:22:31,116
[narrator]<i> The cultural institutions</i>
<i>of Puerto Rico</i>

519
00:22:31,183 --> 00:22:33,085
<i>enjoy an international reputation.</i>

520
00:22:33,152 --> 00:22:34,320
-[Servo] For omelets.

521
00:22:37,423 --> 00:22:40,659
[narrator]<i> All over the island,</i>
<i>abundant recreational facilities</i>

522
00:22:40,726 --> 00:22:43,195
<i>await the visitor, old and young.</i>

523
00:22:43,262 --> 00:22:44,530
-Like swing sets!

524
00:22:46,799 --> 00:22:50,536
[narrator]<i> Championship golf courses,</i>
<i>the finest to be found anywhere.</i>

525
00:22:50,603 --> 00:22:51,737
-[Servo] Are in Florida.

526
00:22:54,039 --> 00:22:55,374
[narrator]<i> The tennis season?</i>

527
00:22:55,441 --> 00:22:56,709
<i>Year round.</i>

528
00:22:56,876 --> 00:22:59,044
-[Mike] Here, Gilligan plays a set.

529
00:23:00,212 --> 00:23:02,815
[narrator]<i> Puerto Rico is</i>
<i>an indoor playground, too.</i>

530
00:23:02,882 --> 00:23:05,718
<i>There's nonstop excitement</i>
<i>both night and day.</i>

531
00:23:05,784 --> 00:23:07,853
-[Servo] In Las Vegas and Atlantic City.

532
00:23:09,822 --> 00:23:11,090
-[Servo and Crow] Gambling!

533
00:23:13,425 --> 00:23:16,328
[narrator]<i> With sunshine</i>
<i>360 days a year,</i>

534
00:23:16,395 --> 00:23:18,597
<i>Puerto Rico offers</i>
<i>something for everyone.</i>

535
00:23:18,664 --> 00:23:19,999
-Like flights out.

536
00:23:20,065 --> 00:23:22,835
[narrator]<i> Fun filled lifestyle,</i>
<i>whatever your pleasure.</i>

537
00:23:23,469 --> 00:23:25,971
-[Mike] Like walking
or holding a tennis racquet.

538
00:23:29,475 --> 00:23:32,077
-[Servo] Spinal injuries are popular
on the island as well.

539
00:23:34,980 --> 00:23:38,918
[narrator]<i> Graceful palms,</i>
<i>an abundance of beautiful beaches.</i>

540
00:23:38,984 --> 00:23:41,453
<i>Puerto Rico is indeed a vacation paradise.</i>

541
00:23:41,520 --> 00:23:43,989
-Abundant with pink and white people.

542
00:23:48,694 --> 00:23:52,031
[narrator]<i> Resorts like Palmas del Mar</i>
<i>blend old world charm</i>

543
00:23:52,097 --> 00:23:53,599
<i>with modern convenience.</i>

544
00:23:53,666 --> 00:23:55,668
-[Mike] Aw, that means no hamburgers.

545
00:23:56,535 --> 00:23:59,872
[narrator]<i> Condominiums, leisure</i>
<i>villages, and a complete range</i>

546
00:23:59,939 --> 00:24:03,509
<i>of outdoor activities make</i>
<i>Progress Island an ideal place</i>

547
00:24:03,575 --> 00:24:06,011
<i>to live for every member of the family.</i>

548
00:24:06,078 --> 00:24:09,782
-[Servo] Except Mom, Dad, brother,
sister, grandpa, grandma.

549
00:24:09,848 --> 00:24:11,050
[music playing]

550
00:24:11,116 --> 00:24:12,217
-[Servo] Ooh!

551
00:24:12,851 --> 00:24:13,953
-[Crow] Hit me!

552
00:24:14,219 --> 00:24:17,523
[narrator]<i> A band concert</i>
<i>under warm, tropical skies.</i>

553
00:24:17,589 --> 00:24:19,925
-The music of John Philip Salsa.

554
00:24:24,363 --> 00:24:26,432
[narrator]<i> For the people here,</i>
<i>the quality of life</i>

555
00:24:26,498 --> 00:24:29,568
<i>is being fulfilled</i>
<i>with each coming year.</i>

556
00:24:29,635 --> 00:24:32,304
<i>It has formed a continuing</i>
<i>pattern of progress</i>

557
00:24:32,371 --> 00:24:35,174
<i>that all started more than</i>
<i>a quarter of a century ago.</i>

558
00:24:38,544 --> 00:24:39,545
-[all] A century ago!

559
00:24:39,611 --> 00:24:43,382
[narrator]<i> In the beginning,</i>
<i>the economy was based on the land itself.</i>

560
00:24:43,916 --> 00:24:45,684
<i>From the time of Columbus,</i>

561
00:24:45,751 --> 00:24:48,387
<i>Puerto Rico has been praised</i>
<i>for the beauty of her landscape.</i>

562
00:24:48,454 --> 00:24:50,956
-[Servo] And sacked and pillaged
many, many times.

563
00:24:52,257 --> 00:24:55,794
[narrator]<i> Hibiscus and bougainvillea</i>
<i>flourish throughout the year.</i>

564
00:24:55,861 --> 00:24:59,398
-[Mike] As do popincolicplooky
and flingilalalafingulala.

565
00:24:59,465 --> 00:25:00,733
[Servo mutters]

566
00:25:00,799 --> 00:25:04,570
[narrator]<i> The mild tropical climate</i>
<i>encourages many forms of agriculture.</i>

567
00:25:04,636 --> 00:25:06,672
-Here are some moo cows.

568
00:25:07,306 --> 00:25:09,775
[narrator]<i> Green bananas</i>
<i>and young pineapples</i>

569
00:25:09,842 --> 00:25:11,510
<i>ripen in the Caribbean sun.</i>

570
00:25:11,577 --> 00:25:13,178
-[Servo] Stop with the sexual imagery!

571
00:25:16,348 --> 00:25:18,317
[narrator]<i> The largest crop is sugar</i>
<i>cane,</i>

572
00:25:18,384 --> 00:25:20,352
<i>which has been cultivated here</i>
<i>for centuries.</i>

573
00:25:20,419 --> 00:25:24,023
-[Mike] So you can sugar frost your damn
corn flakes, filthy American pigs.

574
00:25:24,089 --> 00:25:28,360
[narrator]<i> Special refineries</i>
<i>process the cane into sugar and molasses.</i>

575
00:25:28,427 --> 00:25:31,864
<i>And thus is born Puerto Rico's</i>
<i>most famous product.</i>

576
00:25:31,964 --> 00:25:33,098
-[Servo and Crow] Mm?
-[narrator]<i> Rum.</i>

577
00:25:33,165 --> 00:25:35,467
-[all cheering]

578
00:25:36,735 --> 00:25:40,205
[narrator]<i> The rums of Puerto Rico,</i>
<i>many different brands,</i>

579
00:25:40,272 --> 00:25:42,875
<i>all with flavors that are</i>
<i>remarkably light and dry.</i>

580
00:25:42,941 --> 00:25:44,443
-[Servo, drunkenly] I love this job.
[hiccup]

581
00:25:44,510 --> 00:25:47,679
[narrator]<i> Almost all of the rum sold</i>
<i>in the US is produced here,</i>

582
00:25:47,746 --> 00:25:49,948
<i>and its export is vital to the economy.</i>

583
00:25:50,015 --> 00:25:52,117
So, drink rum constantly.

584
00:25:53,519 --> 00:25:56,722
[narrator]<i> Within this building</i>
<i>is the heartbeat of that economy,</i>

585
00:25:56,789 --> 00:25:59,091
<i>the Economic Development Administration,</i>

586
00:25:59,158 --> 00:26:02,561
<i>which has achieved remarkable progress</i>
<i>through industrial growth.</i>

587
00:26:02,628 --> 00:26:04,396
-[Mike] Here comes the hard sell.

588
00:26:04,463 --> 00:26:06,498
[narrator]<i> Operation Bootstrap,</i>

589
00:26:06,565 --> 00:26:10,202
<i>a pioneering program for self-help</i>
<i>developed by the agency,</i>

590
00:26:10,269 --> 00:26:12,471
<i>has created an investment environment</i>

591
00:26:12,538 --> 00:26:14,807
<i>which attracts industry</i>
<i>from around the world.</i>

592
00:26:14,973 --> 00:26:16,642
-You mean cheap labor.

593
00:26:16,708 --> 00:26:18,444
-[Mike] Even "sss-p-sss" is here.

594
00:26:21,647 --> 00:26:24,950
-[Servo]<i> ♪ Strawberries, marshmallows</i>
<i>Tinfoil, and fudge ♪</i>

595
00:26:25,017 --> 00:26:28,253
<i>♪ And some dingle balls</i>
<i>Hanging from trees ♪</i>

596
00:26:29,321 --> 00:26:32,224
-Why, even aliens from Mars are here.

597
00:26:32,291 --> 00:26:34,726
[narrator]<i> A skilled workforce</i>
<i>makes Puerto Rico</i>

598
00:26:34,793 --> 00:26:37,362
<i>the largest manufacturer</i>
<i>of many computers,</i>

599
00:26:37,429 --> 00:26:39,565
<i>and a leader in solid state technology.</i>

600
00:26:39,631 --> 00:26:42,601
-[Mike] Things with blinky,
buttony-type things.

601
00:26:44,470 --> 00:26:46,605
-[narrator]<i> Pharmaceuticals...</i>
-[Servo and Crow] Whoo!

602
00:26:46,672 --> 00:26:49,041
[narrator]<i> ...that demand the highest</i>
<i>standards of quality and control.</i>

603
00:26:49,108 --> 00:26:52,311
-When Judy Garland died,
it destroyed Puerto Rico's economy.

604
00:26:52,377 --> 00:26:55,214
[narrator]<i> With the help of</i>
<i>a generous tax incentive program,</i>

605
00:26:55,280 --> 00:26:57,616
<i>hundreds of businesses,</i>
<i>both large and small,</i>

606
00:26:57,683 --> 00:26:59,518
<i>have grown and prospered here.</i>

607
00:26:59,585 --> 00:27:01,019
-[Mike] Even the Pink Jeans Company.

608
00:27:01,086 --> 00:27:03,622
[narrator]<i> Manufacturing includes</i>
<i>everything from apparel...</i>

609
00:27:03,689 --> 00:27:05,757
[Servo humming]
[narrator]<i> ...to high technology products</i>

610
00:27:05,824 --> 00:27:07,059
<i>for the home and industry.</i>

611
00:27:07,126 --> 00:27:09,361
-[Servo] She's plucking her chin hairs!
Yuck.

612
00:27:09,428 --> 00:27:11,230
-Then KISS came to town!

613
00:27:13,832 --> 00:27:17,836
-[Mike] We don't know what this is, folks,
but it's definitely Puerto Rico stuff.

614
00:27:19,805 --> 00:27:22,040
[narrator]<i> One of the most</i>
<i>important operations</i>

615
00:27:22,107 --> 00:27:25,043
<i>on Progress Island...</i>
-[Servo beatboxing to soundtrack]

616
00:27:25,110 --> 00:27:27,779
[narrator]<i> ...is petrochemicals. Nearly</i>
<i>two billion dollars has been invested</i>

617
00:27:27,846 --> 00:27:28,847
<i>in plants and facilities.</i>

618
00:27:28,914 --> 00:27:31,083
-[Servo] And millions poisoned.

619
00:27:31,150 --> 00:27:33,152
[Servo beatboxing to music]

620
00:27:33,218 --> 00:27:34,987
-Another fun friend!

621
00:27:36,755 --> 00:27:39,391
-[Mike] Complete with giant rolls
of toilet paper.

622
00:27:39,458 --> 00:27:40,826
[narrator]<i> Whatever the industry,</i>

623
00:27:40,893 --> 00:27:44,329
<i>Puerto Rico's greatest natural resource</i>
<i>is her people.</i>

624
00:27:44,396 --> 00:27:45,998
-[Servo] Come exploit her people!

625
00:27:48,800 --> 00:27:52,337
-[Mike] Shoo-de-doo.
These will never go out of style.

626
00:27:52,404 --> 00:27:54,473
-Comes complete with
a Peter Frampton album.

627
00:27:56,775 --> 00:27:59,678
[narrator]<i> A growing volume of goods</i>
<i>from the continental United States</i>

628
00:27:59,745 --> 00:28:03,282
<i>arrives in Puerto Rico,</i>
<i>now the fifth largest market</i>

629
00:28:03,348 --> 00:28:05,918
<i>in the world for US products and services.</i>

630
00:28:05,984 --> 00:28:08,654
-[Servo] The fifth or 18th or something.

631
00:28:09,755 --> 00:28:12,191
[narrator]<i> Customers here</i>
<i>on Progress Island</i>

632
00:28:12,257 --> 00:28:15,594
<i>mean over 130,000 jobs</i>
<i>throughout all the 50 states.</i>

633
00:28:15,661 --> 00:28:16,662
-[Crow giggling]

634
00:28:16,728 --> 00:28:18,897
[narrator]<i> And thus,</i>
<i>contribute billions of dollars</i>

635
00:28:18,964 --> 00:28:21,066
<i>to the nation's economy.</i>
-[Servo] Oh.

636
00:28:21,133 --> 00:28:23,068
[narrator]<i> Puerto Rico</i>
<i>and the United States,</i>

637
00:28:23,602 --> 00:28:25,304
<i>a partnership for progress.</i>

638
00:28:25,370 --> 00:28:27,472
-[Mike] Whether you like it or not.

639
00:28:28,240 --> 00:28:30,509
-[Crow] Ahh!
OK, we'll move there!

640
00:28:30,576 --> 00:28:33,712
-[Mike] Can you dig it, can you dig it
can you dig it, can you dig it?

641
00:28:33,779 --> 00:28:35,647
[narrator]<i> An America democracy.</i>

642
00:28:35,714 --> 00:28:36,848
-[Mike] Are we starting over?

643
00:28:36,915 --> 00:28:38,684
[narrator]<i> Island of old world elegance.</i>

644
00:28:40,185 --> 00:28:42,921
<i>Rich in its heritage</i>
<i>of art and culture.</i>

645
00:28:42,988 --> 00:28:44,489
-Ooh, look at the art.

646
00:28:44,556 --> 00:28:47,092
[narrator]<i> A wealth</i>
<i>of historic grandeur and beauty.</i>

647
00:28:47,159 --> 00:28:48,627
-[Servo] Is found on other islands.

648
00:28:51,330 --> 00:28:53,865
[narrator]<i> The Spaniards named it</i>
<i>"Puerto Rico".</i>

649
00:28:53,932 --> 00:28:55,767
-[Mike] Because they couldn't
think of anything else.

650
00:28:55,834 --> 00:28:57,769
[narrator]<i> Today, we call it...</i>

651
00:28:57,836 --> 00:28:58,904
-[Crow] Cheap Laborville.

652
00:28:58,971 --> 00:29:00,639
[narrator]<i> ...Progress Island, USA.</i>

653
00:29:00,706 --> 00:29:04,243
-[Servo] Because we have no regard for
history, culture, or tradition.

654
00:29:04,309 --> 00:29:07,346
-[Mike] We would like to apologize
to all the people of Puerto Rico

655
00:29:07,412 --> 00:29:09,047
that we did not offend.

656
00:29:09,815 --> 00:29:10,816
Bye-bye.

657
00:29:24,997 --> 00:29:26,231
-[Servo humming]

658
00:29:26,298 --> 00:29:28,433
-Ha.

659
00:29:28,634 --> 00:29:31,069
-This is nice, isn't it?
-Yep.

660
00:29:31,136 --> 00:29:32,237
-Yeah.

661
00:29:33,372 --> 00:29:35,807
[rock music playing]

662
00:29:35,874 --> 00:29:37,042
-What the heck could that be?

663
00:29:37,109 --> 00:29:39,211
-I don't know.
Whatever it is, it's annoying.

664
00:29:39,278 --> 00:29:41,647
Hey, Cambot, show me
Rocket Number nine, will you?

665
00:29:41,713 --> 00:29:44,683
[cheering and rock music]

666
00:29:44,750 --> 00:29:46,051
-Hey, come on, keep it down!

667
00:29:46,118 --> 00:29:47,519
-[Servo] Yeah, you bunch
of water buffalo!

668
00:29:47,586 --> 00:29:48,687
-What's your problem?

669
00:29:48,754 --> 00:29:50,956
-Well, we were just wondering, you know,
if you could keep it down.

670
00:29:51,023 --> 00:29:52,190
-What do you mean?

671
00:29:52,257 --> 00:29:54,092
I mean, what's your deal?
Don't you party?

672
00:29:54,159 --> 00:29:55,227
-Yeah, party!

673
00:29:55,294 --> 00:29:57,129
-Woo! Party!
Yeah, we party all the time.

674
00:29:57,195 --> 00:29:58,230
-Come on.

675
00:29:58,297 --> 00:30:00,198
-Because you can come over
anytime, and it's no big deal.

676
00:30:00,265 --> 00:30:02,134
-Really?
-You know what, you guys?

677
00:30:02,200 --> 00:30:04,336
I was just thinking,
when we graduate,

678
00:30:04,403 --> 00:30:06,238
I'm not gonna see
you guys anymore.

679
00:30:06,305 --> 00:30:08,307
And I love you guys so much.

680
00:30:08,373 --> 00:30:09,508
-Shut up, Shelly!

681
00:30:09,574 --> 00:30:12,577
-Well, we were just wondering, you know,
if you could pipe down.

682
00:30:12,644 --> 00:30:13,779
-Pipe!
-[cheering]

683
00:30:13,845 --> 00:30:15,180
-Bad choice of words, Mike.

684
00:30:15,247 --> 00:30:18,483
-Oh, my God, you guys, Doug gets off
in about a half an hour

685
00:30:18,550 --> 00:30:20,585
at the Pump and Munch.
I gotta get ready, all right?

686
00:30:20,652 --> 00:30:21,720
Keep partying!

687
00:30:21,787 --> 00:30:24,423
-Hey, here's my two bucks!
Who do I pay?

688
00:30:24,489 --> 00:30:26,224
-[groan]
-What the hell?

689
00:30:26,291 --> 00:30:29,961
-Hey, you wanna stick around
and party after everyone goes?

690
00:30:30,028 --> 00:30:31,396
-Hey, come on over, guys.

691
00:30:31,463 --> 00:30:33,231
They got Rick Wakeman's
<i>Journey to the Center of the Earth!</i>

692
00:30:33,298 --> 00:30:34,299
[cheering]

693
00:30:34,366 --> 00:30:37,135
-Crow, come on. Get back here.
This is so depressing.

694
00:30:37,202 --> 00:30:38,603
This is like
something out of our--

695
00:30:38,670 --> 00:30:39,838
-Out of our youth, maybe?

696
00:30:39,905 --> 00:30:41,106
I know just what to do, Mike.

697
00:30:41,173 --> 00:30:42,841
[whistling siren noises]

698
00:30:42,908 --> 00:30:44,309
-I smell bacon!

699
00:30:44,376 --> 00:30:45,644
Escape!

700
00:30:46,311 --> 00:30:47,979
-[laughing]
-That's pretty good.

701
00:30:48,046 --> 00:30:51,049
-Whoa. Geez, Mike, what have
you got against partying?

702
00:30:51,116 --> 00:30:52,718
-Hey, I party--
We'll be right back.

703
00:30:52,784 --> 00:30:54,419
I party as much as the next guy.
-Look what I got.

704
00:30:54,486 --> 00:30:55,854
-You got a tattoo!
-Yeah!

705
00:30:58,023 --> 00:30:59,791
[ticking clock]

706
00:30:59,858 --> 00:31:02,260
-[Crow] I was having so much fun.
-[Servo] Wild man.

707
00:31:02,327 --> 00:31:03,829
-[Mike] Well, maybe we'll party
sometime.

708
00:31:03,895 --> 00:31:07,432
-[Servo] Shabbily furnished room action
like you've never seen it before.

709
00:31:08,834 --> 00:31:10,202
-I'm Mike Wallace.

710
00:31:10,268 --> 00:31:11,503
-[Mike] I'm Morley Safer.

711
00:31:11,570 --> 00:31:12,904
-[Servo] I'm Ed Bradley.

712
00:31:13,739 --> 00:31:17,008
- Odds are she's eating
ramen noodles tonight.

713
00:31:17,509 --> 00:31:19,478
-[Mike]<i> ♪ Woke up, got out of bed ♪</i>

714
00:31:19,544 --> 00:31:23,048
<i>♪ Dragged a comb across my head ♪</i>

715
00:31:23,548 --> 00:31:26,218
-I'd be depressed
were it not for my room.

716
00:31:30,522 --> 00:31:31,723
-[Servo] Joey Ramone!

717
00:31:33,191 --> 00:31:35,160
-[Mike] Well, it's really
not worth screaming.

718
00:31:35,227 --> 00:31:36,461
What's the point?

719
00:31:37,863 --> 00:31:40,132
-The Cindy Williams murders.

720
00:31:42,467 --> 00:31:44,436
-[Mike] OK, I'll give you my bio notes!

721
00:31:47,105 --> 00:31:48,240
[ticking stops]

722
00:31:48,573 --> 00:31:50,809
-[Mike] Oh, it was her who was ticking.

723
00:31:52,811 --> 00:31:55,580
Let me tuck you in.
You've had a hard day of dying.

724
00:31:58,283 --> 00:32:00,352
Just, uh, show the folks my butt there.

725
00:32:04,089 --> 00:32:05,857
-[Servo] Presents, Scarlet.

726
00:32:06,525 --> 00:32:08,059
Maybe not. I don't think so.

727
00:32:09,494 --> 00:32:12,197
-[Mike] Abbot and Costello meet
the Beast of Yucca Flats.

728
00:32:12,264 --> 00:32:14,766
-I wish it was
the breast of Yucca Flats.

729
00:32:17,436 --> 00:32:19,738
-[Servo] You think Coleman Francis
had a casting couch?

730
00:32:19,805 --> 00:32:22,040
-[Mike] Probably a casting cot.

731
00:32:23,341 --> 00:32:24,443
Lost Boys?

732
00:32:30,382 --> 00:32:33,985
-Oh, so the beast
pilot's in in his own Piper Cub.

733
00:32:37,889 --> 00:32:39,524
-[Mike] Oh.
-[Servo] Huh.

734
00:32:39,591 --> 00:32:41,793
I figured Tor Johnson
would play the butler.

735
00:32:41,927 --> 00:32:43,428
-[Mike] Tor Johnson as the Beast.

736
00:32:43,495 --> 00:32:44,996
That's just smart casting.

737
00:32:45,063 --> 00:32:46,164
-[Crow] Mm.

738
00:32:46,231 --> 00:32:49,000
Coleman Francis actually
had a staff for this movie.

739
00:32:49,067 --> 00:32:50,335
Can you believe it?

740
00:32:50,402 --> 00:32:52,304
-[Servo] Ah, Progress Island.

741
00:32:53,338 --> 00:32:55,474
-[Mike] Get off my land, you credits.

742
00:32:57,476 --> 00:33:00,879
-[Servo]<i> ♪ Never be</i>
<i>Your Beast of Yucca Flats ♪</i>

743
00:33:01,179 --> 00:33:02,280
Thank you.

744
00:33:06,051 --> 00:33:08,386
-[Mike] Masterpiece Theater presents.

745
00:33:09,788 --> 00:33:12,591
-When in Hollywood,
visit Francisland.

746
00:33:14,459 --> 00:33:17,462
-[Servo] This doesn't look like
the Coleman Francis genre, does it?

747
00:33:17,529 --> 00:33:20,432
[narrator]<i> Joseph Javorsky,</i>
<i>noted scientist.</i>

748
00:33:20,499 --> 00:33:21,533
-[Mike] And airplane.

749
00:33:21,600 --> 00:33:24,569
[narrator]<i> Recently escaped</i>
<i>from behind the Iron Curtain.</i>

750
00:33:24,636 --> 00:33:26,972
<i>Wife and children killed in Hungary.</i>

751
00:33:27,439 --> 00:33:29,441
<i>His aide carries a briefcase--</i>

752
00:33:29,508 --> 00:33:31,810
<i>secret data on the Russian moon shot.</i>

753
00:33:31,877 --> 00:33:33,011
-[Crow] And a change of underpants.

754
00:33:33,078 --> 00:33:36,982
[narrator]<i> Joseph Javorsky's</i>
<i>destination-- Yucca Flats,</i>

755
00:33:37,082 --> 00:33:40,519
<i>and a meeting with top brass</i>
<i>at the A-bomb testing grounds.</i>

756
00:33:40,585 --> 00:33:42,521
-[Servo] Avon testing grounds?
-[Crow] Huh.

757
00:33:42,587 --> 00:33:44,289
[Servo] Rabbits with little shaved
butts.

758
00:33:46,291 --> 00:33:49,361
-[Mike] Oh, wait. I'm sorry.
That's just Tor Johnson.

759
00:33:50,562 --> 00:33:52,330
-[Crow] Mitch Jenkins pulls up.

760
00:33:52,397 --> 00:33:55,200
Destination, Yucca Flats Super Red.

761
00:33:56,968 --> 00:33:59,771
-[Servo] Time for go
to ground transportation.

762
00:34:01,072 --> 00:34:02,340
-Here's the airport, Mr. Khrushchev.

763
00:34:02,407 --> 00:34:04,409
Well, not really an airport.
More of a field.

764
00:34:04,476 --> 00:34:06,545
A landing strip.
Well, not a strip.

765
00:34:06,611 --> 00:34:09,481
-[Mike] Marlon Brando arrives for
the Larry King interview.

766
00:34:09,548 --> 00:34:11,149
-[Servo] Jeez, even his head
has a beer belly.

767
00:34:11,216 --> 00:34:13,552
[narrator]<i> These men are also</i>
<i>from behind the Iron Curtain.</i>

768
00:34:13,618 --> 00:34:14,719
-Pay no attention to them.

769
00:34:14,786 --> 00:34:17,989
[narrator]<i> Two of the Kremlin's</i>
<i>most ruthless agents.</i>

770
00:34:18,056 --> 00:34:19,357
<i>Their orders?</i>

771
00:34:20,325 --> 00:34:21,960
<i>Get the briefcase.</i>

772
00:34:22,994 --> 00:34:24,229
<i>Kill Javorsky.</i>

773
00:34:24,296 --> 00:34:25,463
-[Mike] They got it all mixed up.

774
00:34:25,530 --> 00:34:28,333
They killed the briefcase
and got Divorsky.

775
00:34:30,769 --> 00:34:32,304
-[Servo] Oh, it's a divining gun.

776
00:34:35,807 --> 00:34:37,976
[airplane flies overhead]

777
00:34:38,043 --> 00:34:40,178
-[Mike] Daddy, what's Vietnam?

778
00:34:40,679 --> 00:34:42,948
-Ooh, he's got a high albedo.

779
00:34:44,783 --> 00:34:45,951
-[Servo] Tony Cardoza!
-[gunshots]

780
00:34:46,017 --> 00:34:47,852
-[Crow] Ew, ew, ew!

781
00:34:50,388 --> 00:34:52,891
-[Mike] This here part doesn't
need any narration, folks.

782
00:34:52,958 --> 00:34:54,759
It stands on its own.

783
00:34:56,394 --> 00:34:57,429
[gunshot]

784
00:34:57,495 --> 00:34:58,930
-[man] Mr. Javorsky, get in the car.

785
00:34:58,997 --> 00:35:01,499
-[Servo] I'm off-camera,
but just do as I say.

786
00:35:03,802 --> 00:35:06,638
-Haven't really
caught the wave of this movie yet.

787
00:35:09,808 --> 00:35:12,043
-[Mike] Who's shooting who
and what should we do?

788
00:35:15,080 --> 00:35:17,782
-[Servo] Beautiful.
Just beautiful.

789
00:35:17,849 --> 00:35:20,952
Off camera excitement,
the Coleman Francis way.

790
00:35:26,291 --> 00:35:28,860
-Johnny, you forgot your lunch!

791
00:35:29,995 --> 00:35:31,296
-[Mike gasps] The coffee guy!

792
00:35:31,363 --> 00:35:32,430
-[Servo] I like coffee.

793
00:35:36,134 --> 00:35:38,303
-They're driving
through a Wisconsin petting zoo!

794
00:35:41,306 --> 00:35:43,708
-[Mike bleating] Help, please.

795
00:35:44,409 --> 00:35:45,510
Help.

796
00:35:46,277 --> 00:35:48,513
-[Servo] Ah, and what would
a Coleman Francis movie be

797
00:35:48,580 --> 00:35:50,548
without the Coleman Francis mountain?

798
00:35:52,884 --> 00:35:55,854
-[Crow] It is more suspenseful
when you don't know what's going on.

799
00:36:01,126 --> 00:36:02,594
[narrator]<i> Flag on the moon.</i>

800
00:36:03,395 --> 00:36:04,763
<i>How did it get there?</i>

801
00:36:05,163 --> 00:36:07,999
-[Mike] These are all just
random sentences, folks.

802
00:36:09,200 --> 00:36:11,269
-[Servo] This does have
all the earmarks of a KGB operation.

803
00:36:11,336 --> 00:36:13,905
[narrator]<i> Secret data.</i>
<i>Pictures of the moon.</i>

804
00:36:13,972 --> 00:36:15,106
-Nude.

805
00:36:16,274 --> 00:36:17,575
-[Mike] I'm ruthless!

806
00:36:18,943 --> 00:36:22,847
[narrator]<i> Secret data,</i>
<i>never before outside the Kremlin.</i>

807
00:36:23,381 --> 00:36:25,383
<i>Man's first rocket to the moon.</i>

808
00:36:25,817 --> 00:36:27,719
-It's the KGB, Mr. Benny!

809
00:36:31,222 --> 00:36:32,957
-[Mike] He's either a ruthless agent

810
00:36:33,024 --> 00:36:34,993
or he's Schemer from
<i>Shining Time Station.</i>

811
00:36:41,433 --> 00:36:44,269
-[Servo] I just love how deep Tony
Cardoza gets into character.

812
00:36:44,335 --> 00:36:46,738
-Yeah, he just goes in there and sits.

813
00:36:46,805 --> 00:36:49,474
-[Mike] He did all his own fake driving
for this movie.

814
00:36:59,951 --> 00:37:03,154
-[Crow] OK, you chase me
for a while. [giggling]

815
00:37:03,221 --> 00:37:04,789
-[Mike] A star city.

816
00:37:08,626 --> 00:37:10,895
[gunshots]

817
00:37:11,529 --> 00:37:12,664
-[Crow] Beautiful.

818
00:37:15,100 --> 00:37:18,436
-[Mike] Coleman Francis,
the cinematic poet of parking.

819
00:37:20,638 --> 00:37:21,639
I'm ruthless!

820
00:37:21,706 --> 00:37:24,442
[gunshots]

821
00:37:25,577 --> 00:37:28,613
[robots and Mike laughing]
-[Crow] I call him dead!

822
00:37:29,981 --> 00:37:31,816
[gunshots]

823
00:37:31,883 --> 00:37:35,120
-[Servo] He's trying things in this
movie he later perfected in<i> Red Zone Cuba.</i>

824
00:37:35,186 --> 00:37:38,923
[gunshots]

825
00:37:38,990 --> 00:37:41,826
-Stay alive, whatever Kurator find.

826
00:37:42,761 --> 00:37:44,028
[gunshots]

827
00:37:44,095 --> 00:37:47,132
-[Mike] Hold on. Just let me
clear out here. I'll just--

828
00:37:47,665 --> 00:37:49,834
-Uh-oh. This darn gun.

829
00:37:53,505 --> 00:37:54,973
Just a sec, people!

830
00:37:55,507 --> 00:37:57,408
-[Servo] Tor be late for interview.

831
00:37:58,510 --> 00:37:59,978
-[Mike] Better call the Kremlin.

832
00:38:00,612 --> 00:38:03,581
-Just don't shoot while I'm reloading!
That wouldn't be fair! OK, OK.

833
00:38:03,648 --> 00:38:06,284
[gunshots]

834
00:38:07,252 --> 00:38:10,021
-[Servo] Man desert horse no name.

835
00:38:10,088 --> 00:38:12,557
[humming]

836
00:38:13,491 --> 00:38:14,526
[gunshot]

837
00:38:15,593 --> 00:38:17,195
-[Mike] Apparently I'm dead.
Ugh.

838
00:38:19,798 --> 00:38:21,833
-Wonder if wife pack lunch.

839
00:38:23,301 --> 00:38:25,136
-[Servo] I'll just hail a cab
and get out of here.

840
00:38:28,039 --> 00:38:29,207
-[Mike] We'll never find him.

841
00:38:29,274 --> 00:38:31,442
Tor just blends right into the foliage.

842
00:38:34,112 --> 00:38:36,915
-Tor was a Navy Seal,
so he can handle this.

843
00:38:37,816 --> 00:38:41,119
-[Servo] No, no, Mrs. Kruschev
has wandered off again.

844
00:38:42,921 --> 00:38:45,824
-[Mike] You know, I thought they
didn't look like Yucca Flatians.

845
00:38:47,392 --> 00:38:50,528
-[Crow in Russian accent] So,
you hear that new Yakov Smirnoff album?

846
00:38:54,632 --> 00:38:56,734
[narrator]<i> Yucca Flats.</i>
-[Servo] I'll say.

847
00:38:57,168 --> 00:38:58,937
[narrator]<i> The A-bomb.</i>
-[Mike] And thou.

848
00:39:02,407 --> 00:39:04,442
-You know, Tor is much like
the Thompson's gazelle.

849
00:39:04,509 --> 00:39:08,780
You know, running, leaping
to elude his predators, and, uh...

850
00:39:09,514 --> 00:39:10,748
[explosion]

851
00:39:10,815 --> 00:39:12,684
-[Servo] Oh, that's what
that button does.

852
00:39:12,750 --> 00:39:15,720
-[Mike] Should we have warned
the nearby town? Nah.

853
00:39:15,787 --> 00:39:17,222
-My Lunchables!

854
00:39:20,091 --> 00:39:22,594
-[Servo] You dare question the great Oz?

855
00:39:28,967 --> 00:39:31,836
-[Servo] Coleman Francis had
a dark, muddy vision.

856
00:39:32,270 --> 00:39:33,404
With some cars.

857
00:39:36,174 --> 00:39:38,543
Yep. Well...

858
00:39:38,610 --> 00:39:40,011
-[Crow] Hm.
-[Servo] Come on.

859
00:39:40,078 --> 00:39:41,546
-[Mike] Get on over here, now.

860
00:39:43,081 --> 00:39:45,383
You know, parallel parking
is easy in Nevada.

861
00:39:45,450 --> 00:39:47,085
You gotta say that for it.

862
00:39:48,453 --> 00:39:52,190
-We could really use another
A-bomb explosion right about now.

863
00:39:53,925 --> 00:39:56,060
-[Servo] See Bonnie and Clyde's death
car!

864
00:39:58,096 --> 00:39:59,797
-[Mike] It was a dark and boring night.

865
00:40:06,337 --> 00:40:08,106
-Carl Sandberg?

866
00:40:08,172 --> 00:40:09,774
[narrator]<i> Vacation time.</i>

867
00:40:09,841 --> 00:40:11,209
<i>Man and wife.</i>

868
00:40:11,976 --> 00:40:13,845
<i>Unaware of scientific progress.</i>

869
00:40:13,912 --> 00:40:16,414
-[Servo] But they know they
need a new dishwasher.

870
00:40:19,484 --> 00:40:21,920
-I told you kids to
settle down back here!

871
00:40:22,153 --> 00:40:24,622
-Oh, wait.
The engine's in the front.

872
00:40:25,590 --> 00:40:28,159
-[Servo] I never said that!
It's so funny you think I would say that.

873
00:40:28,226 --> 00:40:29,661
I never said that.

874
00:40:30,461 --> 00:40:31,496
-[Mike] Wait, don't tell me.

875
00:40:31,562 --> 00:40:34,866
Tor Johnson, you old
son of a so and so!

876
00:40:36,367 --> 00:40:38,770
-I wonder if I look like Laura Dern.

877
00:40:38,836 --> 00:40:39,938
Nah.

878
00:40:40,004 --> 00:40:41,272
No.

879
00:40:41,339 --> 00:40:42,507
No.

880
00:40:42,974 --> 00:40:44,776
-[Servo] OK, OK, I'm dead already.

881
00:40:44,842 --> 00:40:46,044
Jeez.

882
00:40:47,812 --> 00:40:51,149
-[Mike] Honey,
we have to be to Izatys by 4:00.

883
00:40:52,750 --> 00:40:55,253
-He's going to use him as a car jack.

884
00:40:56,988 --> 00:40:59,157
-[Servo] There was no such thing
as clinical depression

885
00:40:59,223 --> 00:41:00,291
until this film was made.

886
00:41:03,628 --> 00:41:05,797
-[Mike] This is a wonderful honeymoon.

887
00:41:11,669 --> 00:41:13,571
-[Servo] Now how'd he get in the back?

888
00:41:14,939 --> 00:41:17,442
-Mom, he's touching me!

889
00:41:18,843 --> 00:41:23,448
-[Mike] Oh, Tor went to some, uh, fancy
club last night and got his hand stamped.

890
00:41:23,514 --> 00:41:25,984
-[Servo] Kenneth Branagh's
Mary Shelley's Bram Stoker's

891
00:41:26,050 --> 00:41:28,720
Wes Craven's Tim Burton's
<i>Beast of Yucca Flats,</i>

892
00:41:28,786 --> 00:41:30,421
a Francis Ford Coppola film.

893
00:41:33,191 --> 00:41:35,293
-[Mike] I'll just sleep till we get
there.

894
00:41:36,427 --> 00:41:38,863
-[Servo] Some marriages
just get off to a rocky start.

895
00:41:41,332 --> 00:41:45,203
-This will not help the
Discover Yucca Flats campaign.

896
00:41:46,637 --> 00:41:49,374
-[Servo humming along with eerie music]

897
00:41:49,440 --> 00:41:52,910
-[Mike] Honey, we at grandma's.

898
00:41:54,012 --> 00:41:56,547
-[Servo] My turn to drive.
I'll just climb over you.

899
00:41:57,949 --> 00:42:00,651
-[Crow] Yep. Look like you need
new alternator light.

900
00:42:03,521 --> 00:42:06,391
-[Mike] Tor went bobbing
for rubber cement again.

901
00:42:07,258 --> 00:42:09,994
-[Servo] Oh, Tor pull huge groin muscle.
Ow.

902
00:42:14,399 --> 00:42:18,036
[narrator]<i> Joseph Javorsky,</i>
<i>noted scientist.</i>

903
00:42:18,102 --> 00:42:20,204
-[Mike] "Melted scientist"?

904
00:42:20,538 --> 00:42:23,274
[narrator]<i> Dedicated his life</i>
<i>to the betterment of mankind.</i>

905
00:42:23,341 --> 00:42:24,742
-And girlkind.

906
00:42:28,179 --> 00:42:30,448
-[Servo] He always did want
a great pair of legs.

907
00:42:32,717 --> 00:42:36,087
-[Mike] You can see why Georgia O'Keefe
loved this part of the country.

908
00:42:36,521 --> 00:42:39,991
-The choreography leaves
something to be desired.

909
00:42:40,058 --> 00:42:43,428
-[Servo] Must get home to catch
<i>Five Mrs. Buchanans.</i>

910
00:42:45,396 --> 00:42:48,032
-[Mike] Never use yourself
to block your tires.

911
00:42:48,099 --> 00:42:49,267
-[Servo] No.

912
00:42:50,034 --> 00:42:51,836
-Well, here's your problem.

913
00:42:51,903 --> 00:42:53,104
You're dead!

914
00:42:54,405 --> 00:42:56,040
-[Servo] I'm Cherokee Jack.

915
00:42:59,977 --> 00:43:01,379
-[Mike] Well, I'm sure he's fine.

916
00:43:01,446 --> 00:43:02,747
I'm sure it's nothing.

917
00:43:04,282 --> 00:43:06,150
-[Crow] I, uh, gotta go tell my mom.

918
00:43:08,920 --> 00:43:11,122
-[Servo] Think, dammit, think!
Must get a car!

919
00:43:11,189 --> 00:43:12,557
Oh.

920
00:43:13,291 --> 00:43:16,594
-[Mike] He leaves to the strains of
the<i> Cherokee Jack </i>theme.

921
00:43:16,661 --> 00:43:18,062
-[Servo humming]

922
00:43:18,129 --> 00:43:20,031
-[Mike]<i> ♪ Cherokee, Cherokee Jack ♪</i>

923
00:43:20,098 --> 00:43:21,532
-[Servo]<i> ♪ Cherokee, Cherokee ♪</i>

924
00:43:21,599 --> 00:43:24,402
-Oh boy. Another great car
approaching scene.

925
00:43:24,469 --> 00:43:28,139
-[Servo] How do you decide when to turn
on your lights in a Coleman Francis movie?

926
00:43:29,307 --> 00:43:31,642
-[Mike] The Bartok music
really makes the scene.

927
00:43:31,709 --> 00:43:33,911
-[Servo]<i> ♪ Cherokee, Cherokee... ♪</i>
[humming]

928
00:43:33,978 --> 00:43:35,947
-[Crow] You know,
this could be Coleman Francis's

929
00:43:36,013 --> 00:43:38,549
very best car approaching sequence ever.

930
00:43:38,616 --> 00:43:40,051
-[Mike] No.
-[Crow] Yes.

931
00:43:40,118 --> 00:43:42,086
-[Servo]
<i>♪ I'll have a cup of coffee and a roll ♪</i>

932
00:43:42,153 --> 00:43:43,921
<i>♪ And maybe just a roll</i>
<i>And maybe then some crullers ♪</i>

933
00:43:43,988 --> 00:43:45,256
<i>♪ And a danish, one or two ♪</i>

934
00:43:45,323 --> 00:43:46,724
[narrator]<i> Young Joe Dobson.</i>

935
00:43:47,892 --> 00:43:49,060
<i>Desert patrolman.</i>

936
00:43:49,127 --> 00:43:51,195
-[Mike] Lover of fine wines.

937
00:43:51,262 --> 00:43:52,997
-[Servo] Finder of lost gloves.

938
00:43:53,731 --> 00:43:55,700
[honking]

939
00:43:59,670 --> 00:44:01,372
-Officer.
-Yeah?

940
00:44:01,439 --> 00:44:04,408
-There's a man dead,
back down the road.

941
00:44:04,475 --> 00:44:05,576
-Dead?

942
00:44:05,643 --> 00:44:07,612
-How far down?
-Couple miles.

943
00:44:07,678 --> 00:44:09,147
Maybe more.

944
00:44:09,680 --> 00:44:11,315
Laying behind his car.

945
00:44:11,749 --> 00:44:13,184
Looks like he's been choked.

946
00:44:13,251 --> 00:44:15,153
-Real actual dialogue.

947
00:44:18,022 --> 00:44:22,426
-[Mike] Fine, I'll just put off breakfast
and investigate your little murder.

948
00:44:22,493 --> 00:44:25,696
[Servo] Oh, no, I can't have a cruller.
I have to look at a body.

949
00:44:25,763 --> 00:44:29,233
-[Mike] Why do I have to go now?
He'll be just as dead after breakfast.

950
00:44:36,007 --> 00:44:37,775
-[Mike] Wow, he's dead.

951
00:44:41,679 --> 00:44:44,282
-[Servo] Oh, it's Westworld.

952
00:44:47,552 --> 00:44:48,886
-OK, car.

953
00:44:48,953 --> 00:44:50,221
Draw.

954
00:44:50,288 --> 00:44:51,722
Oh.

955
00:44:51,789 --> 00:44:56,260
-[Mike] What the hell?
It's got a Nash front and a VW back.

956
00:44:56,794 --> 00:44:59,764
-[Servo] Good thing I came around.
Battery could've died.

957
00:45:03,968 --> 00:45:05,136
-Oh, my God.

958
00:45:05,203 --> 00:45:09,106
In all my years on the force,
I've never seen a flat tire like that.

959
00:45:10,641 --> 00:45:13,444
-[Mike] Sir, do you have any idea
how fast you were dying?

960
00:45:16,614 --> 00:45:17,648
[narrator]<i> Joe Dobson.</i>

961
00:45:17,715 --> 00:45:19,183
-[Servo] Bitter over missing breakfast.

962
00:45:19,250 --> 00:45:21,285
[narrator]
<i>Caught in the wheels of progress.</i>

963
00:45:21,352 --> 00:45:24,689
-[Mike] Having trouble logging on
to the information superhighway.

964
00:45:28,626 --> 00:45:31,629
-I'm still trying to figure out
that "flag on the moon" thing.

965
00:45:31,696 --> 00:45:33,264
-[Mike] I hear you.

966
00:45:33,331 --> 00:45:35,733
-[Servo] I'll have to ask the interior
to step out of the car, please.

967
00:45:37,902 --> 00:45:40,571
-[Mike] Hey, a little bit of melted
leftover Kit-Kat bar.

968
00:45:40,638 --> 00:45:42,373
[narrator]<i> Man choked to death.</i>

969
00:45:42,440 --> 00:45:44,942
-[Mike] On a purse.
-[narrator]<i> A woman's purse.</i>

970
00:45:45,910 --> 00:45:47,878
<i>And footprints on the wasteland.</i>

971
00:45:48,212 --> 00:45:50,147
-[Mike] His limericks aren't very good.

972
00:45:54,118 --> 00:45:55,319
-[Servo] Watch out for snakes!

973
00:46:00,858 --> 00:46:02,793
-[Mike] Nope, breakfast calls.

974
00:46:06,530 --> 00:46:08,065
-Fine, thanks.

975
00:46:08,132 --> 00:46:09,867
Just leave me here.

976
00:46:11,035 --> 00:46:13,504
-[Servo] Let's see now. Let's see.
Should I have French toast, or--

977
00:46:13,571 --> 00:46:15,106
No! The blockbuster breakfast.

978
00:46:15,172 --> 00:46:19,277
Yeah. Ooh, heaps of hash browns,
sweet, flaky pastries, some sausages.

979
00:46:19,343 --> 00:46:21,145
Yum, yum, yum, yum.

980
00:46:22,813 --> 00:46:25,783
[narrator]<i> Touch a button, things</i>
<i>happen.</i>

981
00:46:25,850 --> 00:46:26,851
-[Mike] Sometimes.

982
00:46:26,917 --> 00:46:29,687
[narrator]<i> A scientist becomes a beast.</i>

983
00:46:29,754 --> 00:46:32,657
-This beast stuff is
harder than I thought.

984
00:46:35,426 --> 00:46:38,195
[Servo] Art thou dead, little cutie?

985
00:46:38,262 --> 00:46:40,731
-[Mike] Hey, I could use a hand here.

986
00:46:41,932 --> 00:46:44,268
-It's now time to make move.

987
00:46:45,503 --> 00:46:46,504
-[Servo] Honey.

988
00:46:46,570 --> 00:46:49,874
Oh, honey, what I wouldn't
do for hair like that.

989
00:46:51,642 --> 00:46:53,477
-[Mike] Hey, Tor, got a match?

990
00:46:53,544 --> 00:46:55,980
-[robots and Mike laugh]
-I love that one.

991
00:46:59,417 --> 00:47:01,952
-Lunch is at 11:30, right, Mike?

992
00:47:02,019 --> 00:47:03,120
-Yeah.

993
00:47:03,187 --> 00:47:05,756
-So is it 11:30, Mike?

994
00:47:06,223 --> 00:47:07,258
-No.

995
00:47:11,395 --> 00:47:13,197
-Now is it 11:30?

996
00:47:13,864 --> 00:47:15,299
-No.

997
00:47:15,366 --> 00:47:18,002
-Well, how soon until 11:30 is it?

998
00:47:18,069 --> 00:47:19,770
-Oh, you don't want to know.

999
00:47:23,641 --> 00:47:25,509
[squeaking]

1000
00:47:30,147 --> 00:47:32,249
-It has to be 11:30 now.

1001
00:47:32,650 --> 00:47:35,419
-It is so not 11:30.

1002
00:47:35,753 --> 00:47:38,189
-It can't not be 11:30.

1003
00:47:38,255 --> 00:47:40,624
-It couldn't be less 11:30.

1004
00:47:41,125 --> 00:47:46,630
-[humming]

1005
00:47:49,500 --> 00:47:52,269
-Is it almost 11:30 now?

1006
00:47:52,670 --> 00:47:55,339
-It is so not 11:30.

1007
00:47:55,940 --> 00:47:58,876
-You can't tell me
it's not 11:30 yet.

1008
00:47:58,943 --> 00:48:02,680
-It has never been less
11:30 than it is right now.

1009
00:48:14,358 --> 00:48:16,026
-Is it 11:30, Mike?

1010
00:48:16,794 --> 00:48:18,829
-Ah, it's completely 11:30.

1011
00:48:18,896 --> 00:48:20,331
-Oh!

1012
00:48:21,732 --> 00:48:23,067
Ha-ha!

1013
00:48:23,734 --> 00:48:24,835
Mm, mm.

1014
00:48:24,902 --> 00:48:26,437
When will it be 11:30 again, Mike?

1015
00:48:26,504 --> 00:48:28,472
-Oh, well, we just went through the--
[movie sign buzzer]

1016
00:48:28,539 --> 00:48:30,508
-Oh, we got movie sign.
-What? What? What?

1017
00:48:30,674 --> 00:48:31,976
-Take your sandwich and come.

1018
00:48:44,488 --> 00:48:46,190
-[Servo] What were you
talking about there?

1019
00:48:46,257 --> 00:48:47,658
-[Mike] I don't remember.

1020
00:48:48,893 --> 00:48:52,763
-[Servo]<i> ♪ Hosanna superstar ♪</i>

1021
00:48:54,598 --> 00:48:57,101
-[Mike] Maybe these people
have some crullers.

1022
00:48:58,235 --> 00:49:00,337
-[Joe] Jim?
-[Crow] Oh?

1023
00:49:00,738 --> 00:49:02,740
-[Jim] Be down in a minute, Joe.

1024
00:49:02,807 --> 00:49:04,909
-She's wearing her Wonder-nightie.

1025
00:49:07,812 --> 00:49:09,847
-[Mike] She's an attractive man.

1026
00:49:09,914 --> 00:49:11,415
[Crow groans]

1027
00:49:12,817 --> 00:49:14,051
-[Servo] Ugh!

1028
00:49:14,118 --> 00:49:16,353
OK, breasts. We get it.

1029
00:49:17,154 --> 00:49:19,990
-[Joe] Yeah.
Better come with me.

1030
00:49:20,057 --> 00:49:22,326
Trouble up the road.
Murder.

1031
00:49:22,393 --> 00:49:23,494
-[Jim] Be right down.

1032
00:49:23,561 --> 00:49:25,896
-I'm glad I can talk to you so openly.

1033
00:49:27,865 --> 00:49:30,801
-[Mike] Oh, uh, flag on the moon, too,
by the way.

1034
00:49:33,137 --> 00:49:34,605
-[Servo] Say hello to your mom.

1035
00:49:36,807 --> 00:49:37,842
-That's a hard face.

1036
00:49:37,908 --> 00:49:40,344
That's a face that challenges you.
-[Servo] Yep.

1037
00:49:43,047 --> 00:49:44,715
-[Jim] See you later, honey.

1038
00:49:45,249 --> 00:49:49,086
-[Mike] Coleman Francis solves
the problem of sound sync.

1039
00:49:49,153 --> 00:49:51,522
[narrator]<i> Jim Archer, Joe's partner.</i>

1040
00:49:51,589 --> 00:49:52,590
-[Servo] Really should wear a shirt.

1041
00:49:52,656 --> 00:49:55,259
[narrator]<i> Another man</i>
<i>caught in the frantic race</i>

1042
00:49:55,326 --> 00:49:58,062
<i>for the betterment of mankind.</i>

1043
00:49:58,762 --> 00:50:00,097
<i>Progress.</i>

1044
00:50:00,631 --> 00:50:04,368
-[Crow] You know, your short window
peepers really hate this house.

1045
00:50:05,803 --> 00:50:07,204
-[Mike] Affordable tract housing.

1046
00:50:07,271 --> 00:50:09,840
-[Servo] That place probably has
an assumable mortgage.

1047
00:50:10,875 --> 00:50:12,543
[narrator]<i> Jim Archer.</i>
-Yo!

1048
00:50:12,610 --> 00:50:14,945
[narrator]<i> Wounded parachuting on Korea.</i>

1049
00:50:15,546 --> 00:50:19,116
<i>Jim and Joe try to keep</i>
<i>the desert roads safe for travelers...</i>

1050
00:50:20,317 --> 00:50:21,819
<i>seven days a week.</i>

1051
00:50:21,886 --> 00:50:24,555
-[Mike] They really wish they
could get some time off.

1052
00:50:26,991 --> 00:50:28,826
-Oh, there he is.
Sorry.

1053
00:50:33,163 --> 00:50:37,368
-[Servo] July 12th, Tor establishes
base camp at the foot of Mount Shasta.

1054
00:50:39,637 --> 00:50:41,739
-[Mike] Now carry over threshold.

1055
00:50:41,805 --> 00:50:44,041
[humming "Here Comes the Bride"]

1056
00:50:47,411 --> 00:50:51,248
-Jeez, a phrenologist would
have a field day with Tor's skull.

1057
00:50:52,349 --> 00:50:54,785
-[Servo] She's not that heavy, but
she's shaped kinda awkward.

1058
00:50:57,221 --> 00:50:59,857
-Here, you take the good boulders.

1059
00:51:02,793 --> 00:51:05,329
-[Servo] It's like
being abducted by Montana.

1060
00:51:05,796 --> 00:51:07,965
[narrator]<i> Shock waves of an A-bomb.</i>

1061
00:51:08,866 --> 00:51:11,569
<i>A once powerful, humble man</i>

1062
00:51:11,635 --> 00:51:14,438
<i>reduced to... nothing.</i>

1063
00:51:14,505 --> 00:51:16,106
-[Mike] But hey, he keeps busy.

1064
00:51:21,579 --> 00:51:25,749
-[Servo] So anyway, you can see
how this all adds up to a movie.

1065
00:51:31,355 --> 00:51:34,024
-I always cry
during these scenes.

1066
00:51:34,825 --> 00:51:37,428
-[Mike] Tor want to make it with you.

1067
00:51:39,229 --> 00:51:41,365
-[Servo] A-ha, Night on Butt Mountain.

1068
00:51:41,432 --> 00:51:43,801
-[Mike] Come up here where
it's really obscene!

1069
00:51:46,570 --> 00:51:48,906
-[Servo] Ricola!

1070
00:51:49,373 --> 00:51:50,541
-[Mike] Love that.

1071
00:51:52,676 --> 00:51:54,578
-[Servo] Tor snaps into action!

1072
00:51:56,914 --> 00:52:00,017
-Sorry, honey.
Hadn't realized you dead.

1073
00:52:01,885 --> 00:52:03,621
-[Servo] I'm gonna play nine holes,
sweetie.

1074
00:52:03,687 --> 00:52:05,122
I'll be back.

1075
00:52:10,694 --> 00:52:12,730
-[Mike] It's a total eclipse of the sun.

1076
00:52:15,499 --> 00:52:16,767
[narrator]<i> Joseph Javorsky.</i>

1077
00:52:16,834 --> 00:52:18,068
-[Crow] Rooty Patooty.

1078
00:52:18,135 --> 00:52:20,104
[narrator]<i> Respected scientist.</i>

1079
00:52:20,170 --> 00:52:21,939
<i>Now a fiend.</i>
-[Servo] But he's my fiend.

1080
00:52:22,006 --> 00:52:24,074
[narrator]<i> Prowling the wastelands,</i>

1081
00:52:24,475 --> 00:52:27,911
<i>a prehistoric beast in the nuclear age.</i>

1082
00:52:28,746 --> 00:52:30,080
<i>Kill.</i>

1083
00:52:30,581 --> 00:52:32,783
<i>Kill just to be killing.</i>

1084
00:52:33,083 --> 00:52:36,153
-[Mike]<i> ♪ Hills alive</i>
<i>Sound of music ♪</i>

1085
00:52:37,921 --> 00:52:42,292
Father Mackenzie, darning his socks
in the night when there's nobody there.

1086
00:52:43,861 --> 00:52:48,332
[narrator]<i> The mouth of the cave,</i>
<i>1,000 feet up over jagged cliffs.</i>

1087
00:52:49,299 --> 00:52:50,968
<i>A man murdered.</i>

1088
00:52:51,568 --> 00:52:53,270
<i>A woman's purse.</i>

1089
00:52:53,704 --> 00:52:56,306
-[Servo] A thin plot, endlessly
restated.

1090
00:52:59,843 --> 00:53:03,414
-[Mike] I think I left a bag of
crullers up here last month.

1091
00:53:04,181 --> 00:53:06,450
-[Servo] This is one tough par five.

1092
00:53:10,487 --> 00:53:13,924
<i>[narrator] Jim and Joe pick their way</i>
<i>upward to the mouth of the cave.</i>

1093
00:53:13,991 --> 00:53:15,826
-[Servo] To fetch a pail of water.

1094
00:53:15,893 --> 00:53:16,927
[narrator]<i> One slip...</i>

1095
00:53:16,994 --> 00:53:18,262
-[Servo] One pair of panties.

1096
00:53:18,328 --> 00:53:20,631
[narrator]<i> ...and 1,000 feet to nowhere.</i>

1097
00:53:20,698 --> 00:53:22,633
-[Mike] I'm sorry,
I meant two feet to nowhere.

1098
00:53:22,700 --> 00:53:23,934
What am I thinking?

1099
00:53:26,603 --> 00:53:29,373
-[Crow] Joe? J-Joe?
Joe, what are you doing to that rock?

1100
00:53:29,440 --> 00:53:30,941
Joe, do not do that.

1101
00:53:31,008 --> 00:53:32,009
Joe.
-[Servo] Buddy.

1102
00:53:34,278 --> 00:53:35,846
Really touchy.

1103
00:53:40,451 --> 00:53:43,987
-[Mike] They haven't gotten very far
with the Crazy Horse monument.

1104
00:53:45,189 --> 00:53:47,124
-[Servo] Hello, mister-- Mr. Johnson?

1105
00:53:47,191 --> 00:53:49,393
Say, hello, Tors, are you home?

1106
00:53:49,460 --> 00:53:52,429
{\an8}[Mike snoring]

1107
00:53:54,364 --> 00:53:56,300
{\an8}-[Joe] Well,
there's the owner of the purse.

1108
00:53:56,366 --> 00:53:58,535
{\an8}-[Jim] Yeah. Let's take her down.

1109
00:53:58,602 --> 00:54:00,270
{\an8}-[Mike] Book her.
-[Jim] May still be alive.

1110
00:54:00,337 --> 00:54:01,772
{\an8}-[Servo] Go ahead, ask her.

1111
00:54:03,440 --> 00:54:05,075
-[Joe] Is she still breathing?

1112
00:54:05,943 --> 00:54:07,911
-[Jim] Careful with her arm, Joe.

1113
00:54:08,612 --> 00:54:11,215
-I told you we should have
brought that two wheeler.

1114
00:54:11,281 --> 00:54:13,417
[Servo grunting]

1115
00:54:13,484 --> 00:54:15,285
-[Mike] Well, if you're going
to stand on her shoulder,

1116
00:54:15,352 --> 00:54:17,654
we're not going to be able
to pick her up, now are we?

1117
00:54:18,055 --> 00:54:20,858
-[Servo] I'm glad Tor didn't cocoon her
with his massive spinnerets.

1118
00:54:23,427 --> 00:54:25,696
-Maybe they should leave a note for Tor.

1119
00:54:26,563 --> 00:54:29,032
-[Servo] Now would be a good time
for some phrases.

1120
00:54:29,099 --> 00:54:30,934
A woman's purse.

1121
00:54:31,001 --> 00:54:32,302
Flag on the moon.

1122
00:54:32,569 --> 00:54:34,338
A man murdered.

1123
00:54:34,404 --> 00:54:36,173
I'll check back in a moment.

1124
00:54:39,042 --> 00:54:42,646
-[Crow] I think this is the single
hardest item on the scavenger hunt.

1125
00:54:43,981 --> 00:54:46,216
-[Jim] Hey, Joe. Here, feel her pulse.

1126
00:54:46,283 --> 00:54:47,551
-[Servo] Isn't that weird?

1127
00:54:48,685 --> 00:54:51,522
-[Joe] Well, doctors can't help her.

1128
00:54:51,922 --> 00:54:54,224
Maybe angels, but not doctors.

1129
00:54:54,291 --> 00:54:56,693
-[Mike] That's kind of
a girly thing to say, Joe.

1130
00:54:57,394 --> 00:54:59,563
Now feel my pulse, Joe.

1131
00:55:00,264 --> 00:55:02,733
-[Servo] She's dead,
so we can just toss her down, right?

1132
00:55:06,036 --> 00:55:07,871
-Paper, mister?
-[Crow] Why, yes it is.

1133
00:55:07,938 --> 00:55:11,341
You're a bright boy. [laughing]

1134
00:55:11,408 --> 00:55:13,811
-[Mike] Did the beast get
mentioned in Winchell?

1135
00:55:14,978 --> 00:55:17,314
-[Servo] Don't give me the floor model,
son. Give me a real one.

1136
00:55:17,381 --> 00:55:20,684
-They don't even have a daily paper,
and they're putting out an extra?

1137
00:55:21,652 --> 00:55:24,621
-[Mike] Kid's gotta sell
all three copies by noon.

1138
00:55:27,858 --> 00:55:30,828
-[Servo] Meanwhile,
in bustling downtown district,

1139
00:55:30,894 --> 00:55:32,763
or "the loop", as they call it.

1140
00:55:33,263 --> 00:55:35,699
-[Mike] Oh, good.
More driving action.

1141
00:55:39,937 --> 00:55:42,139
[narrator]<i> Vacation time.</i>
-So good bye.

1142
00:55:42,206 --> 00:55:46,710
[narrator]<i> People travel east,</i>
<i>west, north, or south.</i>

1143
00:55:46,777 --> 00:55:49,746
-[Servo] Some people just burrow
straight down, I guess.

1144
00:55:49,813 --> 00:55:52,282
[narrator]<i> With two small boys.</i>

1145
00:55:52,850 --> 00:55:54,484
<i>Adventurous boys.</i>

1146
00:55:54,551 --> 00:55:57,221
-[Mike] Boys who swordfight
in the desert sand.

1147
00:55:57,287 --> 00:55:59,590
[narrator]<i> Nothing bothers some people.</i>

1148
00:55:59,656 --> 00:56:01,158
<i>Not even flying saucers.</i>

1149
00:56:01,225 --> 00:56:02,359
-[Crow] What the-- huh?

1150
00:56:03,393 --> 00:56:05,562
-[Mike] I forgot what I came here for.

1151
00:56:07,164 --> 00:56:09,666
-Come on, Art.
Let's get some soda pop.

1152
00:56:10,234 --> 00:56:11,735
-[Servo] Check their oil, Lassie.

1153
00:56:11,802 --> 00:56:14,004
-[man] Hey, mister?
-[Mike] Can I have another doggie chew?

1154
00:56:14,071 --> 00:56:16,139
-[man] Uh, do you mind if we
bother you a minute?

1155
00:56:17,741 --> 00:56:20,677
-Well, as long as I don't
have to speak on camera.

1156
00:56:20,744 --> 00:56:22,479
[Mike] Here you go, doggie.

1157
00:56:23,280 --> 00:56:25,282
[grunting]

1158
00:56:25,349 --> 00:56:26,850
-[Servo] Tales of the gold monkey.

1159
00:56:28,018 --> 00:56:29,453
-[Mike] It's Tetanus Park!

1160
00:56:29,519 --> 00:56:30,988
[narrator]<i> Boys from the city,</i>

1161
00:56:31,488 --> 00:56:34,758
<i>not yet caught</i>
<i>in the whirlwind of progress,</i>

1162
00:56:35,626 --> 00:56:38,061
feed soda pop to the thirsty pigs.

1163
00:56:38,128 --> 00:56:40,130
-Pigs go better with Coke.

1164
00:56:41,131 --> 00:56:43,133
-[Servo] Pigs begin writing
on the side of the barn.

1165
00:56:46,036 --> 00:56:47,371
[narrator]<i> Coyotes.</i>

1166
00:56:47,804 --> 00:56:50,507
<i>Once a menace to travelers.</i>

1167
00:56:51,141 --> 00:56:53,644
<i>Missile bases run them</i>
<i>off their hunting ground.</i>

1168
00:56:53,710 --> 00:56:55,612
-That's another movie I'm working on.

1169
00:56:55,679 --> 00:56:56,747
-[Servo] Humphrey!

1170
00:56:58,215 --> 00:56:59,516
-How much do I owe you?

1171
00:56:59,583 --> 00:57:01,318
-$3.00.

1172
00:57:01,618 --> 00:57:03,954
-[woman] Boys!
We're leaving!

1173
00:57:04,021 --> 00:57:06,957
-[Mike] We're going to the ear wax
museum.

1174
00:57:07,024 --> 00:57:08,792
-You shouldn't have killed that pig.

1175
00:57:08,859 --> 00:57:09,960
-[Servo whispers] Sorry.

1176
00:57:11,695 --> 00:57:13,797
-Mom, we saw some real pigs.

1177
00:57:14,097 --> 00:57:15,599
-Yeah, and a coyote.

1178
00:57:15,666 --> 00:57:17,434
-Coyote?
-Yeah.

1179
00:57:17,501 --> 00:57:19,503
-Don't you be playing with coyotes.

1180
00:57:19,569 --> 00:57:22,506
Now come on.
We're leaving. Come on.

1181
00:57:22,572 --> 00:57:24,942
-Aw, we never get to have any fun.

1182
00:57:25,008 --> 00:57:27,311
-[Mike] But you looked at pigs.
And there's a tire.

1183
00:57:27,377 --> 00:57:28,845
You've had lots of fun.

1184
00:57:31,782 --> 00:57:34,217
-[Servo] So long, folks.
Enjoy our bleak landscape.

1185
00:57:41,491 --> 00:57:43,660
-How'd she get away from us?
She's dead.

1186
00:57:43,727 --> 00:57:46,530
-Well, she just gave me the slip, Joe.

1187
00:57:47,464 --> 00:57:49,833
[narrator]<i> 110 in the shade...</i>

1188
00:57:49,900 --> 00:57:52,602
-[Servo] 130 in my pants.
[narrator]<i> ...and no shade.</i>

1189
00:57:52,903 --> 00:57:56,106
<i>Jim and Joe try to make</i>
<i>their way up to the plateau.</i>

1190
00:57:56,974 --> 00:57:59,977
<i>To reach the top,</i>
<i>a man needs an airplane.</i>

1191
00:58:00,043 --> 00:58:01,345
-Or a big pogo stick.

1192
00:58:01,411 --> 00:58:04,715
[narrator]<i> A jump from a plane</i>
<i>could land you on top.</i>

1193
00:58:05,882 --> 00:58:07,351
-[Mike] Or a helicopter might do.

1194
00:58:07,417 --> 00:58:09,853
Did I say "flag on the moon" yet?

1195
00:58:10,587 --> 00:58:12,456
-[Servo] Why do they call this "flats"?

1196
00:58:12,522 --> 00:58:15,192
-I get the yucca part, but--

1197
00:58:15,258 --> 00:58:18,428
-[Servo] Oh, this is not good
for my groin injury! Ow.

1198
00:58:19,429 --> 00:58:20,998
-[Mike] Thank you for holding me, Ray.

1199
00:58:21,064 --> 00:58:22,566
You have soft hands.

1200
00:58:25,435 --> 00:58:28,105
Jeez, his star is the size
of a dinner plate.

1201
00:58:28,171 --> 00:58:29,172
[robots laugh]

1202
00:58:31,942 --> 00:58:35,612
-The beast put $100 down
and bought an old Studebaker.

1203
00:58:36,913 --> 00:58:40,150
-[Servo] Yucca Flats Frontier Days
is every August, by the way.

1204
00:58:40,217 --> 00:58:42,819
And the Yucca Flats Jaycees
are very active.

1205
00:58:45,389 --> 00:58:48,158
-[Mike] Maybe it's some other folks
caught up in the progress.

1206
00:58:48,225 --> 00:58:51,128
[narrator]<i> But the killer's</i>
<i>not on the plateau.</i>

1207
00:58:51,194 --> 00:58:54,297
-The killer's in Memphis,
marrying his 12-year-old cousin.

1208
00:58:57,067 --> 00:58:59,369
-[Servo] Javorsky. Ron Javorsky.

1209
00:58:59,436 --> 00:59:01,438
Played quarterback for the Eagles.

1210
00:59:02,973 --> 00:59:04,674
-[Mike] Well, I don't know
what we were doing

1211
00:59:04,741 --> 00:59:07,778
or why we climbed those rocks,
but here we are.

1212
00:59:08,845 --> 00:59:11,681
[narrator]<i> Hours in the broiling hot</i>
<i>desert sun,</i>

1213
00:59:12,416 --> 00:59:13,884
<i>with no trace of the killer.</i>

1214
00:59:13,950 --> 00:59:15,285
-Phooey.

1215
00:59:15,352 --> 00:59:20,223
[narrator]<i> To put Jim Archer's paratrooper</i>
<i>training to good use is the only answer.</i>

1216
00:59:20,290 --> 00:59:21,558
-[Servo] Here, have a little propane.

1217
00:59:21,625 --> 00:59:24,694
[narrator]<i> A trip up into the skies</i>
<i>and jump.</i>

1218
00:59:24,761 --> 00:59:26,063
-[Mike] Start flapping your arms.

1219
00:59:26,129 --> 00:59:30,400
[narrator]<i> And if the killer</i>
<i>is on the plateau, kill him.</i>

1220
00:59:30,467 --> 00:59:33,904
-Plus, Coleman has lots
of skydiving footage.

1221
00:59:35,472 --> 00:59:38,275
-[Servo] Come on, faster! Faster!
We're not in the air yet!

1222
00:59:43,046 --> 00:59:44,648
-[tire pops loudly]
-[Crow] Whoa!

1223
00:59:44,714 --> 00:59:46,716
A sudden attack of drunkenness sets in!

1224
00:59:50,020 --> 00:59:52,089
-[Mike] Right into your living room.
-[Servo] Whoa.

1225
00:59:55,492 --> 00:59:58,595
-[Servo] We invite you to visit
Yucca Flats for the beautiful fall colors.

1226
00:59:58,962 --> 01:00:00,597
-Hey, how'd they get in Cuba?

1227
01:00:03,433 --> 01:00:06,336
-[Mike] I'm going put
my little star on here. This could be--

1228
01:00:10,540 --> 01:00:13,610
-[Servo] Ah! That must be the Yucca
Flats.

1229
01:00:13,677 --> 01:00:14,778
-[laughs]
-[Mike groans]

1230
01:00:14,845 --> 01:00:16,179
-[Crow groans]

1231
01:00:16,246 --> 01:00:17,447
-[woman] Hank?

1232
01:00:17,514 --> 01:00:19,616
I don't see the boys.

1233
01:00:20,217 --> 01:00:21,485
-[Hank] I'll take a look.

1234
01:00:21,551 --> 01:00:23,520
-[Mike] Well,
they're not in the tire, honey.

1235
01:00:26,456 --> 01:00:29,192
-That is why the close-up was invented.

1236
01:00:31,761 --> 01:00:32,762
-[Servo] Sheriff Tintin.

1237
01:00:33,330 --> 01:00:35,465
-Jim, shoot first.

1238
01:00:35,932 --> 01:00:37,467
Ask questions later.

1239
01:00:37,534 --> 01:00:39,903
-[Mike] They're just little boys
who wandered off!

1240
01:00:43,840 --> 01:00:46,109
-The camera loves me.

1241
01:00:47,210 --> 01:00:49,679
-[Servo] The pubescentest gun in the
West.

1242
01:00:56,086 --> 01:00:57,554
-[Mike] Good luck, boingy.

1243
01:01:01,491 --> 01:01:04,594
<i>-♪ Those mediocre men</i>
<i>In their flying machines ♪</i>

1244
01:01:04,661 --> 01:01:06,062
-[Servo]<i> ♪ They sort of go up ♪</i>

1245
01:01:06,129 --> 01:01:08,365
<i>♪ They kind of go uppity up, up ♪</i>

1246
01:01:08,431 --> 01:01:09,566
-There you go.

1247
01:01:13,737 --> 01:01:15,505
[Mike hums to soundtrack]

1248
01:01:15,572 --> 01:01:18,008
<i>♪ Yucca Flats ♪</i>

1249
01:01:18,074 --> 01:01:20,410
<i>♪ Yucca Flats ♪</i>

1250
01:01:20,477 --> 01:01:23,780
-[Crow] Hank, I can't find me now.

1251
01:01:24,481 --> 01:01:28,185
-[Servo] A very nice prairie dog
taught me about being a woman.

1252
01:01:28,919 --> 01:01:31,154
-[Mike] What was I looking for?
-Hank?

1253
01:01:31,221 --> 01:01:32,622
I can't find them.

1254
01:01:33,623 --> 01:01:35,292
What do you think we should do?

1255
01:01:35,358 --> 01:01:37,861
-I don't know, Lois.
-[Mike] I'm not Hank. Does that help?

1256
01:01:37,928 --> 01:01:39,496
-Stay here. I'll go out farther.

1257
01:01:39,829 --> 01:01:41,031
We'll find them.

1258
01:01:41,932 --> 01:01:44,701
-Worrying about the kids
helps keep intimacy at bay.

1259
01:01:48,305 --> 01:01:50,173
-[Servo] Ah, we're up in the air!
Oh!

1260
01:01:52,776 --> 01:01:55,278
-Thank God for the Steadicam, huh?

1261
01:01:57,614 --> 01:01:59,049
-[Mike] Nope, no reaction.

1262
01:01:59,115 --> 01:02:00,517
Doesn't do it for me. Nope.

1263
01:02:04,621 --> 01:02:06,723
-[Servo] I love an old gold man.

1264
01:02:11,194 --> 01:02:12,529
-[Lois] Randy?

1265
01:02:13,163 --> 01:02:14,164
Art?

1266
01:02:14,231 --> 01:02:16,866
-The dingos took my baby.

1267
01:02:17,467 --> 01:02:21,071
-[Servo]<i> ♪ I feel pretty</i>
<i>Oh so pretty ♪</i>

1268
01:02:24,107 --> 01:02:25,375
-[Crow] Coleman gets arty!

1269
01:02:25,942 --> 01:02:29,479
-[Mike] You know, these two
do make a crack rescue team.

1270
01:02:32,048 --> 01:02:34,517
-[Servo] Wow! We could pee anywhere.

1271
01:02:34,584 --> 01:02:37,053
-Ron and Clint Howard in<i> True West.</i>

1272
01:02:37,988 --> 01:02:40,257
-[Mike] Sorry,
I still can't feel anything.

1273
01:02:40,323 --> 01:02:41,992
Nope. Nothing.

1274
01:02:46,830 --> 01:02:49,799
-[Servo] Jeez, the first
McDonald's Playlands weren't that good.

1275
01:02:51,768 --> 01:02:54,170
-What are they staring at?
Have they seen Christ?

1276
01:02:56,973 --> 01:02:59,676
-[Mike] I thought Shrubworld
would be more fun.

1277
01:03:04,781 --> 01:03:07,917
-Unstoppable parking action!
Whoo!

1278
01:03:07,984 --> 01:03:10,553
-[Servo] Marvel as they
get out of the car.

1279
01:03:11,988 --> 01:03:15,358
-[Servo]
<i>♪ There's a bright golden haze on the-- ♪</i>

1280
01:03:25,835 --> 01:03:27,103
-Art?

1281
01:03:27,837 --> 01:03:28,872
Randy?

1282
01:03:28,938 --> 01:03:30,807
-I'm feeling randy!

1283
01:03:31,941 --> 01:03:33,176
-[Servo] You Randy?

1284
01:03:33,243 --> 01:03:34,678
Nah.

1285
01:03:34,744 --> 01:03:36,279
-[Mike] Ah, hell, I put in enough time.

1286
01:03:37,981 --> 01:03:39,249
-Hey, a raccoon!

1287
01:03:47,023 --> 01:03:50,193
-[Mike] This whole sequence
can't go on long enough for me.

1288
01:03:53,496 --> 01:03:54,798
-[Servo]<i> ♪ Hosanna ♪</i>

1289
01:03:55,398 --> 01:03:59,969
<i>♪ Hosanna superstar ♪</i>

1290
01:04:00,403 --> 01:04:02,439
-[Mike whispers] Stop that.
-[Servo whispers] Sorry.

1291
01:04:02,505 --> 01:04:05,208
-It's Nickelodeon's<i> Waiting for Godot.</i>

1292
01:04:07,711 --> 01:04:10,447
-[Servo] What an oddly proportioned
little man, huh?

1293
01:04:12,816 --> 01:04:17,687
-[Crow] It was daring of Coleman to
change the theme from parking to walking.

1294
01:04:17,754 --> 01:04:19,089
-[Mike] The beast! Ah!

1295
01:04:19,155 --> 01:04:20,690
No.
-[Crow] Oh.

1296
01:04:20,757 --> 01:04:23,159
-[Servo] I'm a lunch lady at heart.

1297
01:04:24,561 --> 01:04:27,831
-I'm poor but clean,
downtrodden but proud.

1298
01:04:29,699 --> 01:04:32,669
-[Servo] This movie stops at nothing,
and stays there.

1299
01:04:33,470 --> 01:04:36,172
-[Mike] Well, at least my shirt
is quite comfortable.

1300
01:04:39,409 --> 01:04:41,544
-Nuclear test site
sure is well guarded.

1301
01:04:41,611 --> 01:04:44,447
-[Servo] It's like he got lost on
his way to the drugstore.

1302
01:04:44,781 --> 01:04:45,882
Huh?

1303
01:04:45,949 --> 01:04:47,283
Hm?

1304
01:04:47,350 --> 01:04:50,120
-[Mike] "Keep... oat.

1305
01:04:50,186 --> 01:04:51,187
Out?

1306
01:04:51,254 --> 01:04:54,424
Go verne mine-t pro-part."

1307
01:04:58,361 --> 01:04:59,896
-Well, on the good side,

1308
01:04:59,963 --> 01:05:01,931
this is time away from Mrs. Potatohead.

1309
01:05:05,502 --> 01:05:09,472
-[Mike] Art? I'm gonna have to make
a lean-to out of your hide.

1310
01:05:10,507 --> 01:05:13,810
-[Servo] Disappointing turnout
for REO Speedwagon at Alpine Valley.

1311
01:05:18,782 --> 01:05:21,351
-Shh. He's laying his eggs.

1312
01:05:22,986 --> 01:05:25,088
-[Mike] Why, it's Verne Gagne.

1313
01:05:26,322 --> 01:05:27,557
[narrator]<i> Always on the prowl.</i>

1314
01:05:27,624 --> 01:05:28,825
-[Servo] For the ladies.

1315
01:05:28,892 --> 01:05:31,761
[narrator]<i> Looking for something</i>
<i>or somebody to kill.</i>

1316
01:05:32,529 --> 01:05:34,497
<i>Quench the killer's thirst.</i>

1317
01:05:35,064 --> 01:05:37,934
-Well, we have mom's looks
and dad's intelligence.

1318
01:05:38,001 --> 01:05:39,836
We should be able to deal with this.

1319
01:05:41,237 --> 01:05:42,739
-[Mike] He looks like a lawn jockey.

1320
01:05:42,806 --> 01:05:44,374
[robots laugh]

1321
01:05:46,576 --> 01:05:49,879
-[Servo]<i> ♪ One singular sensation ♪</i>

1322
01:05:49,946 --> 01:05:51,815
[humming]

1323
01:05:56,119 --> 01:05:57,120
-[Servo] Huh?

1324
01:05:57,187 --> 01:05:58,955
-Tor, you've lost weight!

1325
01:06:03,626 --> 01:06:05,829
-[Mike] This guy is stealing this film.

1326
01:06:06,229 --> 01:06:08,298
-[Servo] Yeah, still can't get worked
up.

1327
01:06:12,135 --> 01:06:14,637
-Nope, nothing over there
excites me either.

1328
01:06:18,808 --> 01:06:21,377
-[Mike] Hey, look!
They opened the new Prange Way.

1329
01:06:24,581 --> 01:06:26,950
[Servo grunting]

1330
01:06:28,218 --> 01:06:29,919
-[Mike] I see a renegade shrub.

1331
01:06:35,458 --> 01:06:37,126
-Hey! Hey, hey, hey!
-[Servo] No no no.

1332
01:06:37,193 --> 01:06:38,228
[gunshot]

1333
01:06:40,230 --> 01:06:43,299
-[Mike] OK, ground, hold still.
-[bullet ricochets]

1334
01:06:43,366 --> 01:06:45,668
-[Servo] No,
don't shoot the dad of Yucca Flats!

1335
01:06:49,272 --> 01:06:52,375
-Vacationing at the Most
Dangerous Game Dude Ranch.

1336
01:06:55,812 --> 01:06:57,480
[gunshot]

1337
01:06:59,215 --> 01:07:01,150
-[Mike] Is that a Piper or a Beechcraft?

1338
01:07:01,518 --> 01:07:02,719
[gunshot]

1339
01:07:03,786 --> 01:07:06,789
-[Servo] This guy's a terrible shot.
No wonder we lost Korea.

1340
01:07:07,757 --> 01:07:09,292
[gunshot]

1341
01:07:09,359 --> 01:07:11,261
[laughter]

1342
01:07:11,327 --> 01:07:12,629
-Oh, owie, owie!

1343
01:07:14,731 --> 01:07:17,567
-[Mike] Oh, thought somebody
killed me there for a second.

1344
01:07:25,775 --> 01:07:26,943
[gunshot]

1345
01:07:27,210 --> 01:07:30,179
-[Servo] Whoa. Guess I'll have to dig
him out of the crack of Butt Mountain.

1346
01:07:30,246 --> 01:07:31,648
[bullets ricochet]

1347
01:07:31,714 --> 01:07:34,484
-What, have they got
a spider monkey holding the camera?

1348
01:07:36,386 --> 01:07:38,855
-[Mike] Feels kind of good.
Weird, but good.

1349
01:07:38,922 --> 01:07:40,423
[Servo laughing]

1350
01:07:43,393 --> 01:07:46,563
-Well, he's a spry little dad, isn't he?

1351
01:07:46,629 --> 01:07:48,498
-[Servo] Is this guy piloting
with his feet?

1352
01:07:48,565 --> 01:07:50,033
-[Crow] Ugh!

1353
01:07:50,099 --> 01:07:51,534
[gunshot]

1354
01:07:53,770 --> 01:07:56,039
-[Mike] Just holding the camera
in front of himself.

1355
01:07:59,809 --> 01:08:01,711
-Well, this doesn't do much for me.

1356
01:08:03,012 --> 01:08:05,214
-[Servo] Oh, he disguised himself
as a tumbleweed.

1357
01:08:05,281 --> 01:08:06,716
See?
-[gunshots]

1358
01:08:13,523 --> 01:08:17,327
-[Mike] At least I'm getting
a good aerobic workout.

1359
01:08:17,393 --> 01:08:20,263
[gunshots]
-[Servo] A bit of a rip-off.

1360
01:08:20,330 --> 01:08:22,231
-[Mike] Coleman steals from only the
best.

1361
01:08:25,601 --> 01:08:28,338
-Oh, this would happen to me!

1362
01:08:30,206 --> 01:08:33,409
-[Servo] Oh, ho-ho, that is hard
on the crotch, I must say.

1363
01:08:34,644 --> 01:08:37,013
-[Mike] Well, he was probably
guilty of something.

1364
01:08:38,381 --> 01:08:40,383
-Wanna get a good look
at that one pebble.

1365
01:08:40,450 --> 01:08:44,087
[narrator]<i> Shoot first,</i>
<i>ask questions later.</i>

1366
01:08:44,153 --> 01:08:46,856
-[Servo] Ask "Christian Slater"?
Okay...

1367
01:08:46,923 --> 01:08:50,460
-[Mike] Shoot the film first,
ask questions later.

1368
01:08:52,161 --> 01:08:54,097
-Great Waffle House down there!

1369
01:09:00,536 --> 01:09:02,305
-[Servo] Wha-- what? Wait, what did I
say?

1370
01:09:03,006 --> 01:09:05,341
-[Mike]<i> ♪ Everything's going my-- ♪</i>

1371
01:09:05,408 --> 01:09:06,809
Well, I'd better get back to--

1372
01:09:09,746 --> 01:09:11,280
[narrator]<i> The pilot dropped his man.</i>

1373
01:09:11,347 --> 01:09:12,715
-[Servo] Now he wants another one.

1374
01:09:12,782 --> 01:09:16,552
[narrator]<i> If Joe Dobson moves north,</i>
<i>Hank will be caught in the middle.</i>

1375
01:09:16,619 --> 01:09:18,254
-Of my constant droning.

1376
01:09:18,321 --> 01:09:21,724
[narrator]<i> An innocent victim,</i>
<i>caught in the wheels of justice.</i>

1377
01:09:21,791 --> 01:09:23,092
-[Mike] I've been handed a rewrite.

1378
01:09:23,159 --> 01:09:26,429
It's no longer "progress".
It's now "justice".

1379
01:09:30,700 --> 01:09:32,669
-[all singing to soundtrack]
<i>♪ Francis ♪</i>

1380
01:09:32,735 --> 01:09:38,641
<i>♪ Coleman Francis</i>
<i>Coleman ♪</i>

1381
01:09:39,942 --> 01:09:41,644
[narrator]<i> A man runs.</i>

1382
01:09:42,111 --> 01:09:43,446
<i>Somebody shoots at him.</i>

1383
01:09:43,513 --> 01:09:45,081
-[Servo] Sorry. That's all we got.

1384
01:09:45,148 --> 01:09:46,883
That's our movie.
Good bye.

1385
01:09:49,052 --> 01:09:51,254
-He's headed for Caesar's Palace.

1386
01:09:52,889 --> 01:09:55,324
-[Mike] Well, still, I'm not with my
wife.

1387
01:09:56,392 --> 01:09:57,994
[Servo] Ow.

1388
01:09:58,061 --> 01:10:00,029
This is not my beautiful gulch!

1389
01:10:01,097 --> 01:10:03,966
-[Crow] I thought they said June 5th!

1390
01:10:05,268 --> 01:10:07,403
-[Mike]<i> The Shootist, </i>with John Wayne.

1391
01:10:10,239 --> 01:10:12,075
-[Servo] Yep, he just landed, folks.

1392
01:10:17,080 --> 01:10:18,514
-What's he doing?

1393
01:10:18,581 --> 01:10:19,782
Oh! [laughs]

1394
01:10:19,849 --> 01:10:23,586
-[Mike] I really should say something
to him about shooting me.

1395
01:10:25,521 --> 01:10:26,589
[narrator]<i> Jim Archer.</i>

1396
01:10:26,656 --> 01:10:29,759
<i>Ex-paratrooper,</i>
<i>trained to hunt down his man.</i>

1397
01:10:29,826 --> 01:10:31,961
-[Servo purrs suggestively]
[narrator]<i> And destroy him.</i>

1398
01:10:32,028 --> 01:10:34,197
-[Mike] And your State Farm
representative.

1399
01:10:35,565 --> 01:10:38,301
-He wakes with a taste for heroin.

1400
01:10:38,367 --> 01:10:41,504
-[Servo]<i> ♪ Hosana ♪</i>

1401
01:10:41,571 --> 01:10:44,941
-[Servo]<i> ♪ Hosanna superstar ♪</i>

1402
01:10:45,007 --> 01:10:47,143
-[Mike] I told you about that.
-[Servo] Sorry.

1403
01:10:47,510 --> 01:10:50,079
-[Mike] Mm, dirt.
Two, three days old.

1404
01:10:51,981 --> 01:10:55,651
-I'm sick, I'm dying, and yet
I have a taste for nachos!

1405
01:10:55,718 --> 01:10:57,920
-[Mike] Mm, nachos.
That's what I need.

1406
01:10:57,987 --> 01:10:59,689
-[Servo]<i> ♪ Hosanna ♪</i>

1407
01:11:12,602 --> 01:11:13,636
-Hello.

1408
01:11:13,703 --> 01:11:15,705
I'm Crow T. Robot
of the Satellite of Love.

1409
01:11:15,772 --> 01:11:20,543
You know,<i> The Beast of Yucca Flats,</i>
<i>Skydivers, </i>and<i> Red Zone Cuba</i>

1410
01:11:20,610 --> 01:11:26,182
are just three examples of the many, many
godawful films made during this century.

1411
01:11:26,249 --> 01:11:30,953
Tragically, films like these
are not deteriorating fast enough.

1412
01:11:31,020 --> 01:11:34,157
That's why I urge you to support FAPS,

1413
01:11:34,223 --> 01:11:36,926
the Film Anti-Preservation Society.

1414
01:11:36,993 --> 01:11:41,230
At FAPS, we're devoted to allowing
the films of Coleman Francis

1415
01:11:41,297 --> 01:11:45,268
and countless others
to die a gentle, natural death.

1416
01:11:45,334 --> 01:11:48,638
We'll use your donations
to transfer these films

1417
01:11:48,704 --> 01:11:51,207
to fragile, volatile silver nitrate stock,

1418
01:11:51,274 --> 01:11:54,110
so they'll rot quickly
into nature's compost.

1419
01:11:54,177 --> 01:11:56,045
Now, here's how you can help.

1420
01:11:56,112 --> 01:12:00,283
If you find a copy of a film as bad as,
oh,<i> Aspen Extreme,</i>

1421
01:12:00,349 --> 01:12:03,820
please, store it in a warm,
moist, salty place

1422
01:12:03,886 --> 01:12:06,455
such as a cheese factory, or your mouth.

1423
01:12:06,522 --> 01:12:08,825
-Uh, Crow?
-The situation is serious.

1424
01:12:08,891 --> 01:12:10,560
If we don't take action now,

1425
01:12:10,626 --> 01:12:13,029
the entire filmography
of Sylvester Stallone

1426
01:12:13,095 --> 01:12:16,899
may be available for our children
and our grandchildren to view.

1427
01:12:16,966 --> 01:12:19,168
So if you want to save future generations

1428
01:12:19,235 --> 01:12:21,103
from the legacy of<i> Cliffhanger,</i>

1429
01:12:21,170 --> 01:12:23,172
<i>Demolition Man, Cobra--</i>
-Crow?

1430
01:12:23,239 --> 01:12:24,240
<i>-Rhinestone.</i>
-Crow?

1431
01:12:24,307 --> 01:12:26,475
<i>-Oscar,</i>
<i>Stop, Or My Mom Will Shoot.</i>

1432
01:12:26,542 --> 01:12:27,677
-Crow?

1433
01:12:27,743 --> 01:12:29,946
-I'm on television, Mike!
<i>Nighthawks, Paradi--</i>

1434
01:12:30,012 --> 01:12:32,849
-Uh, don't you think what
you're suggesting is wrong?

1435
01:12:33,850 --> 01:12:35,718
-You're right, Mike.
How awful of me.

1436
01:12:35,785 --> 01:12:36,986
Where was I?

1437
01:12:37,053 --> 01:12:39,522
So please, call now,
and pledge what you can.

1438
01:12:39,589 --> 01:12:42,325
Just dial 1-800-LETROT.

1439
01:12:42,391 --> 01:12:43,893
Won't you?
Thank you.

1440
01:12:43,960 --> 01:12:47,396
-[laughing] Yeah, Crow T. Robot
may or may not be back.

1441
01:12:47,463 --> 01:12:49,699
Uh, just come right this way.
Could you?

1442
01:12:49,765 --> 01:12:51,534
-Important film meeting?
-Yeah, that's right.

1443
01:12:51,601 --> 01:12:53,736
-I'm ready for my close up.
Mr. Katzenberg, hello!

1444
01:12:53,803 --> 01:12:54,937
-[Mike] You look great.

1445
01:13:00,109 --> 01:13:01,811
-[Mike] Love this movie.

1446
01:13:01,878 --> 01:13:05,081
-[Servo] Oh, boy.
I'm always surprised to see human feces.

1447
01:13:05,147 --> 01:13:07,049
[narrator]<i> The hunter and the hunted.</i>

1448
01:13:07,116 --> 01:13:08,851
-[Mike] This fall on ABC.

1449
01:13:08,918 --> 01:13:11,888
[narrator]<i> With only a few hundred yards</i>
<i>between him and the enemy.</i>

1450
01:13:11,954 --> 01:13:14,023
<i>Jim closes in for the kill.</i>

1451
01:13:14,090 --> 01:13:16,092
-[Servo] And just as Jim
closes in for the kill,

1452
01:13:16,158 --> 01:13:19,095
so does Mutual of Omaha
close in for the kill.

1453
01:13:19,161 --> 01:13:20,863
[Mike snickers]

1454
01:13:24,400 --> 01:13:28,371
-She was in Bergman film
where she played low self-esteem.

1455
01:13:28,738 --> 01:13:30,439
-[Mike] Today's Romanian woman.

1456
01:13:33,676 --> 01:13:35,344
-[Servo] Well, let's see.
Was I shot in the gut?

1457
01:13:35,411 --> 01:13:37,346
Or, no, the leg.
No, the arm, I think.

1458
01:13:37,413 --> 01:13:40,283
Well, some part of me hurts.
I'm not sure which. Ow.

1459
01:13:43,119 --> 01:13:46,422
-Harcourt Fenton Mudd,
you dirty, rotten, lazy thing,

1460
01:13:46,489 --> 01:13:48,190
thing, thing!

1461
01:13:48,758 --> 01:13:52,094
-[Mike] Bob Mathias is Chet Baker
in<i> The Jesse Owens Story.</i>

1462
01:13:52,929 --> 01:13:54,463
-[Servo] Oh, my muffins!
Oh my.

1463
01:13:57,500 --> 01:14:00,536
-Honey, I'm taking the kids
off the find list.

1464
01:14:00,603 --> 01:14:01,837
-[Lois] Where are the boys?

1465
01:14:01,904 --> 01:14:02,905
Why are you running?

1466
01:14:02,972 --> 01:14:05,608
-I have no time to explain, Lois.
Stay here.

1467
01:14:05,675 --> 01:14:06,976
The boys may come back.

1468
01:14:07,043 --> 01:14:08,077
I've gotta get help.

1469
01:14:08,144 --> 01:14:10,513
-Uh, but-- but-- but--
what-- don't leave!

1470
01:14:10,579 --> 01:14:13,582
I mean, c-can I have the canteen?

1471
01:14:13,883 --> 01:14:16,052
Oh, oh.

1472
01:14:16,118 --> 01:14:18,154
-[Mike] Lads, he is a wondrous mystery.

1473
01:14:20,990 --> 01:14:24,360
-Honey, my abandonment issues
are coming up!

1474
01:14:25,161 --> 01:14:27,596
[narrator]<i> Joe Dobson headed north</i>
<i>and met Jim.</i>

1475
01:14:27,663 --> 01:14:29,465
-[Mike] They married and settled down.

1476
01:14:31,100 --> 01:14:32,234
-[Servo] Well, gotta go!

1477
01:14:36,939 --> 01:14:39,075
-Hi. Shoot any joggers today?

1478
01:14:39,141 --> 01:14:41,644
-[Servo sings to soundtrack]<i> ♪ Beast of</i>
<i>Yucca Flats, beast of, beast of ♪</i>

1479
01:14:41,711 --> 01:14:43,079
<i>♪ Beast of Yucca Flats ♪</i>

1480
01:14:43,145 --> 01:14:46,315
[narrator]<i> Twenty hours without rest</i>
<i>and still no enemy.</i>

1481
01:14:46,382 --> 01:14:47,650
-[Mike] He has an S body.

1482
01:14:47,717 --> 01:14:49,385
[narrator]<i> In the blistering desert</i>
<i>heat,</i>

1483
01:14:49,452 --> 01:14:51,954
<i>Jim and Joe plan another attack.</i>

1484
01:14:52,021 --> 01:14:53,022
-[Servo] On what?

1485
01:14:53,089 --> 01:14:55,591
[narrator]<i> Find the beast and kill him.</i>

1486
01:14:55,658 --> 01:14:57,293
-OK, it's the same plan.

1487
01:14:57,360 --> 01:14:59,295
-[Servo purrs suggestively]
-[Mike] Ha-ha-ha.

1488
01:14:59,362 --> 01:15:02,331
[narrator]<i> Man's inhumanity to man.</i>

1489
01:15:02,398 --> 01:15:05,167
-[Servo] Or beast's inhubeastity to
beast.

1490
01:15:09,372 --> 01:15:11,240
-I'm blocking out the fact
that they shot me.

1491
01:15:11,307 --> 01:15:13,576
I'm not letting it get to me.

1492
01:15:15,244 --> 01:15:17,413
-[Servo] Oh, would you open the gate,
please?

1493
01:15:17,480 --> 01:15:19,715
I'm near death, for crying out--
Oh, jeez.

1494
01:15:25,021 --> 01:15:28,924
-[Mike] The fifth grade production
of<i> Of Mice And Men.</i>

1495
01:15:33,662 --> 01:15:36,098
[Servo hums to soundtrack]

1496
01:15:36,165 --> 01:15:37,400
-[Crow] Oh, there.

1497
01:15:37,466 --> 01:15:39,635
That feels a lot better.
I'm glad I did that.

1498
01:15:39,702 --> 01:15:41,504
Oh, no one-- no one will see that.

1499
01:15:41,570 --> 01:15:43,372
-[Servo]<i> Heidi, </i>the mature years.

1500
01:15:44,440 --> 01:15:45,708
-[Art] Randy?

1501
01:15:45,775 --> 01:15:47,376
You think we're lost?

1502
01:15:47,443 --> 01:15:49,812
-[Mike] You can't keep things from kids,
huh?

1503
01:15:49,879 --> 01:15:51,714
-[Randy] I don't know, Art.

1504
01:15:51,781 --> 01:15:53,616
Maybe. Let's go.

1505
01:15:53,682 --> 01:15:56,419
-[Crow] Those Donner party people
seem very nice.

1506
01:15:58,788 --> 01:15:59,822
Taxi?

1507
01:16:03,959 --> 01:16:07,830
-[Servo] Well, I suppose I'll just
sit here and wait for death to take me.

1508
01:16:09,198 --> 01:16:11,734
-[Mike] I'll sew.
That's what I'll do.

1509
01:16:13,836 --> 01:16:15,638
[Servo] Oh, honey.

1510
01:16:15,704 --> 01:16:18,808
-[Crow] Well, I'll permit myself
one Chiclet now.

1511
01:16:19,875 --> 01:16:23,646
-[Servo] I've been meaning to clean my
glasses for months, but you get so busy.

1512
01:16:24,547 --> 01:16:25,848
-[Mike] I'm sad.

1513
01:16:29,585 --> 01:16:31,520
-[Crow] Many years later.

1514
01:16:32,354 --> 01:16:34,690
-[Servo] So, did Francis promise you
back end, too?

1515
01:16:37,726 --> 01:16:41,397
-[Mike] Remarkably, the boys stumble
across the source of the Nile.

1516
01:16:44,266 --> 01:16:46,469
-[Crow] The concept of Wham! is born.

1517
01:16:47,937 --> 01:16:49,371
-[Art] Randy?

1518
01:16:49,438 --> 01:16:51,440
Is that water down there?

1519
01:16:51,507 --> 01:16:53,409
-[Randy] I sure hope so.

1520
01:16:53,476 --> 01:16:55,111
-[Art] My throat's dry.

1521
01:16:55,177 --> 01:16:56,512
-[Randy] Mine too.

1522
01:16:56,579 --> 01:16:59,915
-[Servo] I've seen water before,
and I'm pretty sure that's water.

1523
01:17:00,416 --> 01:17:02,384
-[Mike] He oughta pop that.
-[Servo] Ugh.

1524
01:17:04,920 --> 01:17:08,757
-[Crow] Should we worry about
the dead cows laying next to the pond?

1525
01:17:10,860 --> 01:17:13,062
-[Servo] Hi. Can I play through?

1526
01:17:14,330 --> 01:17:16,665
-[Mike] Those butts are ripe for
kicking.

1527
01:17:17,900 --> 01:17:20,169
-[Crow] Uh, well, it wasn't water.

1528
01:17:20,236 --> 01:17:21,971
-[Servo] Oh, cute crop top.

1529
01:17:26,208 --> 01:17:28,544
-[Mike] Well, we're in Wisconsin now.

1530
01:17:28,911 --> 01:17:32,281
Or maybe it's Cuba.
Or Nevada. Whatever.

1531
01:17:32,348 --> 01:17:34,884
-[howling]

1532
01:17:34,950 --> 01:17:37,453
-[Crow] Oh, the young Ed McMahon.

1533
01:17:38,821 --> 01:17:40,222
-[Servo] Cut it out, Mom.

1534
01:17:43,292 --> 01:17:46,395
-[Mike] I must pose
for my Dustbowl portrait.

1535
01:17:47,696 --> 01:17:52,034
-Back in '54, there were
many gentleman callers I had.

1536
01:17:52,401 --> 01:17:54,637
-[Mike] Where did they find
my third grade teacher?

1537
01:17:56,472 --> 01:17:59,074
-[Servo] I'm pretty sure this is a shot
intended to convey something.

1538
01:18:01,110 --> 01:18:03,479
-[Crow] This is an odd sort of stalking.

1539
01:18:05,748 --> 01:18:07,583
-[Mike] I swallowed a bug.

1540
01:18:07,650 --> 01:18:09,919
-[Crow] He must have dropped
a bowl of Cream of Wheat on his head

1541
01:18:09,985 --> 01:18:11,253
and it hardened.

1542
01:18:11,320 --> 01:18:12,488
[all chuckle]

1543
01:18:12,555 --> 01:18:15,224
-[Servo] I think Tor would be
more comfortable in a skirt, don't you?

1544
01:18:15,291 --> 01:18:16,525
-[Mike shudders]

1545
01:18:17,626 --> 01:18:19,828
Is that yellow tiger tail?

1546
01:18:19,895 --> 01:18:22,131
-[Crow] Oh, would you close your mouth?

1547
01:18:22,598 --> 01:18:24,533
-[growling]
-[Crow imitates growl]

1548
01:18:24,600 --> 01:18:26,202
-[growling]

1549
01:18:26,268 --> 01:18:30,072
-[Servo laughs] You know, for Tor,
sneaking up and pursuit are not his forte.

1550
01:18:33,142 --> 01:18:36,445
-[Mike] You know, Tor was originally
offered the role of the mom in this film.

1551
01:18:41,283 --> 01:18:43,886
[Servo beatboxing to soundtrack]

1552
01:18:44,787 --> 01:18:47,356
-[Servo] Oh, I think she's going
to do an impression.

1553
01:18:49,491 --> 01:18:51,660
-After a brief moment of almost action,

1554
01:18:51,727 --> 01:18:53,395
we're back to our normal pace.

1555
01:18:57,032 --> 01:18:59,735
-[Servo] Flag on the moon.
How'd it get there?

1556
01:18:59,802 --> 01:19:01,971
[whispers] What the hell does that mean?

1557
01:19:03,038 --> 01:19:07,509
-[Mike] You know, this place is probably
nicer than Javorsky's place in Moscow.

1558
01:19:07,576 --> 01:19:08,777
-[Art] Randy?

1559
01:19:08,844 --> 01:19:09,979
-[Randy] What?

1560
01:19:10,045 --> 01:19:11,413
-[Art] Where are we?

1561
01:19:11,480 --> 01:19:12,815
-[Randy] In a cave, I guess.

1562
01:19:12,881 --> 01:19:15,251
-[Servo scoffs]
-Come on, Art.

1563
01:19:15,317 --> 01:19:16,385
Be careful.

1564
01:19:16,452 --> 01:19:18,187
-[Mike] Randy?
Do we state the obvious?

1565
01:19:18,254 --> 01:19:21,423
-[Art] Think that old man
can get us up here?

1566
01:19:21,490 --> 01:19:24,059
-[Randy] Don't talk about it.
Come on.

1567
01:19:24,126 --> 01:19:27,796
-[Crow] You're blocking, Randy.
We have issues to deal with.

1568
01:19:28,697 --> 01:19:32,134
-[Mike] Mm-hm.
How the hell did I get up here?

1569
01:19:32,868 --> 01:19:36,905
-[Servo]<i> ♪ High on hill</i>
<i>Live lonely goatherd, lay lay loo ♪</i>

1570
01:19:36,972 --> 01:19:40,276
-[Crow] Tor is all the Brothers Johnson
rolled into one.

1571
01:19:43,145 --> 01:19:45,414
-[Randy] Art, get back.

1572
01:19:45,481 --> 01:19:48,150
-[Mike] Oh, false alarm. It's not Mom.

1573
01:19:49,518 --> 01:19:52,254
-[grunting]

1574
01:19:52,321 --> 01:19:56,292
-[Servo] Why?! Why can't I get
a baked potato before 5:00 in this town?

1575
01:19:57,226 --> 01:20:00,396
-[continues grunting]

1576
01:20:03,699 --> 01:20:05,768
-[Crow] They never should have
let Tor improv.

1577
01:20:05,834 --> 01:20:08,804
-[Servo and Mike imitate grunting]

1578
01:20:14,977 --> 01:20:18,047
[narrator]<i> The beast,</i>
<i>finding his victim gone,</i>

1579
01:20:18,113 --> 01:20:19,581
<i>unleashes his fury.</i>

1580
01:20:20,316 --> 01:20:22,451
-[Mike] Aw, fudge-- [mutters].

1581
01:20:22,518 --> 01:20:23,852
-[yelling]

1582
01:20:23,919 --> 01:20:25,587
-[Crow] To the moon, Alice!

1583
01:20:32,695 --> 01:20:37,166
-[Servo] Tor could have been contender
instead bum which am.

1584
01:20:37,866 --> 01:20:40,035
-Tor have tough day.

1585
01:20:41,370 --> 01:20:44,673
-[Mike] He always does a few
push-ups before turning in.

1586
01:20:45,307 --> 01:20:47,943
-[Servo] Beautiful night.
Tor sleep on porch.

1587
01:20:50,379 --> 01:20:54,550
-[Mike grunts]
With the thing and the-- ah!

1588
01:20:54,616 --> 01:20:57,453
Yeah.
[continues grunting]

1589
01:21:01,590 --> 01:21:03,559
-[Servo] Think we can jump him, Randy?

1590
01:21:06,228 --> 01:21:09,331
-Oh, wait.
Tor forget to set alarm.

1591
01:21:10,866 --> 01:21:14,837
-[Servo] The only landscape I know
that's enhanced by telephone poles.

1592
01:21:14,903 --> 01:21:17,172
-[Mike] Would it hurt
the Atomic Energy Commission

1593
01:21:17,239 --> 01:21:19,541
to stop by once in a while to say hello?

1594
01:21:19,608 --> 01:21:22,010
-[Randy] Art, come on.

1595
01:21:24,079 --> 01:21:26,448
-[grunts]
-[Randy] Get back!

1596
01:21:26,515 --> 01:21:28,617
-[Crow] What is he, a beast,
or my<i> mashuguna </i>grandpa?

1597
01:21:28,684 --> 01:21:31,120
-[Servo] You kids with your bikes.
Ugh!

1598
01:21:32,521 --> 01:21:36,592
-[Mike] If Hank is gone seven years,
I'm free to marry again.

1599
01:21:37,526 --> 01:21:40,496
-[Crow] Waiting for the romance between
Tor and Mom to get going.

1600
01:21:40,562 --> 01:21:42,030
-[Mike] Yeah, that's a twist.

1601
01:21:46,068 --> 01:21:48,404
[narrator]<i> With Hank</i>
<i>and some helpful neighbors...</i>

1602
01:21:48,470 --> 01:21:50,606
-[Servo] They raise a barn.
-[narrator]<i> ...the search narrows.</i>

1603
01:21:50,672 --> 01:21:54,376
-[Mike] They're concentrating on the rogue
Russian scientists in the neighborhood.

1604
01:21:55,110 --> 01:21:57,446
-[Crow barking]

1605
01:21:59,181 --> 01:22:00,482
[Servo meows]

1606
01:22:00,549 --> 01:22:03,118
-I'm ready for my closeup, Mr. Francis.

1607
01:22:04,019 --> 01:22:07,556
-[Mike] Now, this could be
vacation footage, for all we know.

1608
01:22:08,824 --> 01:22:12,094
-And the Tasty Freeze staff
is brought in to help.

1609
01:22:15,664 --> 01:22:19,468
-[Servo] What are you,
with your rap, and... [groaning]

1610
01:22:20,302 --> 01:22:22,504
-[Mike] Don't turn off the game!
I was watching that!

1611
01:22:25,774 --> 01:22:27,943
-Argh, I'll teach you kids!

1612
01:22:30,112 --> 01:22:32,648
-[Servo] The tension is so thick,
you could cut it with a knife,

1613
01:22:32,714 --> 01:22:34,016
but, please, use a spoon.

1614
01:22:36,585 --> 01:22:38,754
-[Mike] Found a<i> Playboy </i>up here once.
Keep your eyes open.

1615
01:22:38,821 --> 01:22:39,988
-[Crow] Really?

1616
01:22:41,223 --> 01:22:43,892
-[Mike] What are you, with your
running and your [shouts].

1617
01:22:46,595 --> 01:22:50,332
-[Crow] With your cactus, and your--
argh!

1618
01:22:51,066 --> 01:22:53,702
-[Servo] Helpful neighbor
shown actual size.

1619
01:22:54,403 --> 01:22:56,572
-[Mike] That bush is trying
to get their attention.

1620
01:23:00,108 --> 01:23:02,244
-[Servo] Moses is lost in the Sinai.

1621
01:23:04,513 --> 01:23:07,649
-You forgot your complementary stick!

1622
01:23:09,051 --> 01:23:11,987
-[Mike] I had a vision I was chased
through the desert by Boog Powell.

1623
01:23:16,158 --> 01:23:18,060
[gunshot]
-[Servo] Ooh!

1624
01:23:18,293 --> 01:23:20,262
Well, it penetrated his back fat.

1625
01:23:24,066 --> 01:23:26,468
[Crow makes rumble noises]

1626
01:23:27,669 --> 01:23:29,905
-[Mike]<i> ♪ We fired our guns</i>
<i>And the British kept a-comin' ♪</i>

1627
01:23:29,972 --> 01:23:31,573
<i>♪ From the Mississippi to-- ♪</i>

1628
01:23:36,178 --> 01:23:39,214
-[Servo] I remember a time when there
were thousands of Tors on the plains.

1629
01:23:39,281 --> 01:23:42,050
Ground would shake.
Plains were black with Tors.

1630
01:23:42,618 --> 01:23:44,953
-Tonight,<i> The Whales of August.</i>

1631
01:23:45,387 --> 01:23:48,290
-[Mike] McCloud, you shot Penn Jillette.

1632
01:23:50,158 --> 01:23:52,995
-Tor is posing for a Reuben's painting.

1633
01:23:54,830 --> 01:23:57,266
-[Servo] Augh! Just kidding.

1634
01:23:58,934 --> 01:24:01,270
-[Mike] Right by
the world's largest Aryan.

1635
01:24:01,336 --> 01:24:02,371
-[Servo laughs]

1636
01:24:02,437 --> 01:24:04,106
-[Mike] Hide him.
-[Servo] Ew.

1637
01:24:06,308 --> 01:24:08,076
-[Crow] Look out! He's rolling!

1638
01:24:10,679 --> 01:24:12,147
-[Servo] Am I late for school?

1639
01:24:14,683 --> 01:24:17,052
-[Mike makes munching noises]

1640
01:24:17,119 --> 01:24:20,289
-[Servo] Another trademark
Coleman Francis butt shot.

1641
01:24:27,696 --> 01:24:30,165
-Balanchine. [giggles]

1642
01:24:32,167 --> 01:24:35,504
-[Mike] Tor's just mad because someone
ripped the Froot Loop off his shirt.

1643
01:24:36,705 --> 01:24:39,875
-[Servo] There, there.
Now try swallowing. Is that better?

1644
01:24:42,511 --> 01:24:44,846
-Tor is a field chiropractor.

1645
01:24:46,248 --> 01:24:48,083
[Mike] Close your mouth.
Quit breathing on me.

1646
01:24:48,150 --> 01:24:49,751
Please, close your mouth.

1647
01:24:52,054 --> 01:24:55,924
-[Servo laughs]
Tor like this! This fun!

1648
01:25:00,662 --> 01:25:03,432
-Hey, gotta get to bed. [laughter]

1649
01:25:03,498 --> 01:25:05,133
[gunshots]

1650
01:25:07,536 --> 01:25:09,771
[gunshots continue]

1651
01:25:13,241 --> 01:25:15,677
-[Crow] Oh, that was wonderful.

1652
01:25:16,712 --> 01:25:19,915
-[Mike] I see the belly
of the great white one!

1653
01:25:21,049 --> 01:25:22,250
-[Servo] J-Jim?

1654
01:25:22,618 --> 01:25:25,087
{\an8}Now, back away slowly
from the crotch.

1655
01:25:25,153 --> 01:25:27,623
{\an8}-[Joe] Jim. You all right?

1656
01:25:27,689 --> 01:25:30,993
{\an8}-[Crow] They're going to split him open
and crawl inside to stay warm.

1657
01:25:31,059 --> 01:25:33,261
{\an8}-[Jim] Yeah, I'm all right.

1658
01:25:33,328 --> 01:25:36,031
{\an8}[Mike] Call me, Tor, hmm?

1659
01:25:36,765 --> 01:25:39,468
-[Servo] They're gonna find
a whole baby deer inside him.

1660
01:25:43,071 --> 01:25:46,241
-This has been one bleak, weird day.

1661
01:25:47,843 --> 01:25:51,380
-[Servo] Hey, about that other guy I shot?
Let's keep that quiet.

1662
01:25:52,547 --> 01:25:55,183
-Come on. Let's go.
-[Mike] We gotta find that beast.

1663
01:25:57,619 --> 01:26:01,490
-[Mike] That's the third beast this week.
We gotta start regulating them.

1664
01:26:02,658 --> 01:26:05,894
-[Servo] Slowly, Tor decays
into his base elements.

1665
01:26:05,961 --> 01:26:08,630
[narrator]<i> Joseph Javorsky.</i>
-[Mike] Aren't you ashamed of yourself?

1666
01:26:08,697 --> 01:26:10,098
[narrator] Noted scientist.

1667
01:26:10,165 --> 01:26:12,801
-Family man,
and your candidate for city council!

1668
01:26:18,540 --> 01:26:20,208
-[Mike] So? What about him?

1669
01:26:25,080 --> 01:26:27,149
-[Servo] Hank, I'm going back to college.

1670
01:26:30,652 --> 01:26:34,022
-[Mike] You know, women today
just can't wait like they used to.

1671
01:26:36,758 --> 01:26:39,294
<i>-The Velveteen Rabbit.</i>

1672
01:26:39,828 --> 01:26:42,597
-[Servo] Isn't it weird that rabbits
have ears that look like antennas?

1673
01:26:45,734 --> 01:26:46,768
-[Mike] That's Trudy.

1674
01:26:49,371 --> 01:26:52,074
-[Mike] Tell me about that last shot
of the rabbits, George.

1675
01:26:52,140 --> 01:26:54,576
-[Art] Mom?
-[Crow] Billy tried to eat my leg.

1676
01:26:54,643 --> 01:26:57,245
-[Art] Mom? Mom?

1677
01:26:57,312 --> 01:27:02,350
-[Servo fake-cries] This is so touching.
-[Mike whispers] It's OK.

1678
01:27:03,485 --> 01:27:05,320
-[Crow] Whoop-whoop-whoop.

1679
01:27:09,391 --> 01:27:13,061
-[Servo] Oh, it's a meat eating rabbit.
-[Crow] And they found the mother lode.

1680
01:27:14,896 --> 01:27:16,131
He's suckling.

1681
01:27:18,200 --> 01:27:20,068
-[Crow] Oh, uh, hi!

1682
01:27:20,135 --> 01:27:22,370
I know what this must look like.

1683
01:27:22,437 --> 01:27:25,240
-You look like my little Ninotschka.

1684
01:27:25,841 --> 01:27:29,411
-[Mike] OK, remain calm. Don't panic.

1685
01:27:30,779 --> 01:27:33,615
-[Servo] And now the bunny eats Tor
and becomes<i> The Night of the Lepus.</i>

1686
01:27:38,153 --> 01:27:41,123
-Hey, I got a man down over here!
Can I get a medic?

1687
01:27:41,189 --> 01:27:43,325
[Servo] Just hold on, buddy.
You're gonna be OK.

1688
01:27:45,260 --> 01:27:48,730
-[Mike] Well, nice to meet you, then.

1689
01:27:48,797 --> 01:27:51,133
-[Crow] Whoo!
That was an incredible ending.

1690
01:27:51,199 --> 01:27:53,034
I just need to sit down for a minute.

1691
01:28:06,915 --> 01:28:08,016
-Yay!
-Whoo-hoo!

1692
01:28:08,083 --> 01:28:10,185
-Oh, what a sweet victory, eh, Crow?
-Ah, sweet.

1693
01:28:10,252 --> 01:28:12,254
-We're going to bring out Mike
in just a moment,

1694
01:28:12,320 --> 01:28:13,755
but first, a little housekeeping.

1695
01:28:13,822 --> 01:28:15,657
We've got a wonderful chicken dinner
waiting for you all,

1696
01:28:15,724 --> 01:28:18,293
so if you'll just hold tight,
we just wanted to read a few

1697
01:28:18,360 --> 01:28:21,129
of the many congratulatory telegrams
that we've been receiving

1698
01:28:21,196 --> 01:28:22,931
over the course of the campaign.
Crow?

1699
01:28:22,998 --> 01:28:25,967
-Ah, here's a congratulary, uh--
Here we go, right here.

1700
01:28:26,034 --> 01:28:29,905
{\an8}This is from Erica Rodriguez Fletcher.
And Erica writes,

1701
01:28:29,971 --> 01:28:33,408
{\an8}"Your show has been a part of our lives
since I was in the fourth grade."

1702
01:28:33,475 --> 01:28:35,377
-[Crow] Wow! Wow!
-"I'm in high school now."

1703
01:28:35,443 --> 01:28:37,779
-"And we still watch your show."
-Wow!

1704
01:28:37,846 --> 01:28:40,515
-"Makes me happy to see our family
laugh their heads off together."

1705
01:28:40,582 --> 01:28:42,384
-Wow!
-"We have a pretty stressful life,

1706
01:28:42,450 --> 01:28:44,886
but you relieve it.
-From us all."

1707
01:28:44,953 --> 01:28:46,621
Ah, thank you. Thank you.
-Isn't that sweet?

1708
01:28:46,688 --> 01:28:48,590
-Thank you, Erica.
-Very sweet.

1709
01:28:48,657 --> 01:28:49,958
--Here's one from Philip White.
--Philip White.

1710
01:28:50,025 --> 01:28:51,827
-White. White. "Greetings, you all.

1711
01:28:51,893 --> 01:28:55,430
I'm not young, artistic, and
precocious, and as not such,

1712
01:28:55,497 --> 01:28:57,599
would not have a snowball's
chance in Minnesota

1713
01:28:57,666 --> 01:29:00,001
of having my letter put on Still Store."
-What?

1714
01:29:00,068 --> 01:29:02,804
-I don't quite get that, but let's see
if we can put-- Oh, no, I'm sorry.

1715
01:29:02,871 --> 01:29:04,339
We can't put it on Still Store. Sorry.

1716
01:29:04,406 --> 01:29:06,074
-Can't put it up there.
-But wait, here comes Mike!

1717
01:29:06,141 --> 01:29:08,677
-It's Mike, everybody!
-Mike Nelson, Mike Nelson!

1718
01:29:08,743 --> 01:29:11,112
Thank you, thank you very much.

1719
01:29:11,179 --> 01:29:12,514
Mike, Mike, Mike, Mike!

1720
01:29:12,581 --> 01:29:14,749
-Thank you. Thank you.

1721
01:29:14,816 --> 01:29:17,285
Thank you very much.
And thank you.

1722
01:29:17,352 --> 01:29:18,687
Thank you. And thank you.

1723
01:29:18,753 --> 01:29:23,258
It was a long and hard campaign,
yet we emerged victorious.

1724
01:29:23,325 --> 01:29:24,492
-[robots] Yay!

1725
01:29:24,559 --> 01:29:26,461
-We tamed<i> The Beast of Yucca Flats.</i>

1726
01:29:26,528 --> 01:29:27,596
-[cheering]

1727
01:29:27,662 --> 01:29:30,398
-We rallied around
the Coleman Francis cry.

1728
01:29:30,465 --> 01:29:32,300
-I'm Cherokee Jack.

1729
01:29:32,367 --> 01:29:35,370
-We took the worst they had to offer,
and we say proudly--

1730
01:29:35,437 --> 01:29:36,571
-Coffee?

1731
01:29:36,638 --> 01:29:38,340
Yes.
I like coffee.

1732
01:29:38,406 --> 01:29:40,842
-With my wife Nancy by my side,

1733
01:29:40,909 --> 01:29:44,079
we overcame a generation
forged in war and discipline

1734
01:29:44,145 --> 01:29:47,883
by a hard and bitter piece of Harry,
England and Saint George.

1735
01:29:47,949 --> 01:29:50,252
We have been to the mountaintop,

1736
01:29:50,318 --> 01:29:52,487
and we heard it say
<i>"Ich ben ein Berlinner,"</i>

1737
01:29:52,554 --> 01:29:54,823
and "Ain't I a woman?"

1738
01:29:59,361 --> 01:30:03,932
-First of all, I'd like to say

1739
01:30:03,999 --> 01:30:08,737
that what we've suffered here tonight
is not a defeat.

1740
01:30:08,803 --> 01:30:10,739
Definitely not a defeat.

1741
01:30:11,006 --> 01:30:14,409
It may look like defeat.

1742
01:30:14,476 --> 01:30:17,545
It may feel like defeat.

1743
01:30:18,146 --> 01:30:20,849
It may even smell like defeat.

1744
01:30:21,917 --> 01:30:23,385
Oh, what the heck.

1745
01:30:23,919 --> 01:30:26,955
Frank, I'm going to
start slapping you now,

1746
01:30:27,022 --> 01:30:28,890
and I may never stop.

1747
01:30:30,358 --> 01:30:32,294
-Let the healing process begin.

1748
01:30:36,364 --> 01:30:38,333
[theme music interposed with slapping]

1749
01:31:40,662 --> 01:31:43,331
{\an8}-[yelling]

